#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179438 :question/id #uuid "622bd0ff-d08a-5504-8b38-8bc388bfcbf5" :question/math-academy-id "e-12525" :question/problem "A particle $P$ of mass $3\\,\\rm{kg}$ moves along the $x$-axis with an acceleration of magnitude $(1 + 2x^2)\\,\\rm{m/s}^2$ acting in the direction $OP.$ Given that $x$ is measured in meters, calculate the work done by the force in moving the particle from $x=0$ to $x=2.$" :question/worked-solution "The work done by a force $F=F(x)$ in moving a particle from $x=x_1$ to $x=x_2$ is given by\n\n$$\nW = \\int_{x_1}^{x_2} F(x) \\,\\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nWhen the force and distance are measured in Newtons and meters, respectively, the work done by the force is measured in Joules ($\\rm{J}$).\n\nUsing Newton's second law of motion, the force required to accelerate a particle of mass $3\\,\\rm{kg}$ at a rate of $(1 + 2x^2)\\,\\rm{m/s}^2$ is\n\n$$\nF = ma = 3 \\cdot (1 + 2x^2) = (3 + 6x^2) \\,\\rm{N}.\n$$\n\nNow, substituting the known values into our integral, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(3+6{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[3x+2{x}^{3}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =3(2)+2({2}^{3})-(3(0)+2({0}^{3})) \\\\  & =6+16-0 \\\\  & =22\\,J.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179439 :question/id #uuid "4e428ee4-0017-5e83-887c-26e723b4af08" :question/math-academy-id "e-12526" :question/problem "A particle $P$ of mass $2\\,\\rm{kg}$ moves along the $x$-axis from rest at the origin with an acceleration of magnitude $(4t+1)\\,\\rm{m/s}^2,$ where the acceleration acts in the direction of increasing $x,$ and $t \\geq 0$ is the time in seconds. Calculate the change in kinetic energy of the particle between the times $t = 1\\,\\rm{s}$ and $t = 2\\,\\rm{s}.$" :question/worked-solution "The change in kinetic energy of a particle with mass $m,$ initial velocity $v_0,$ and final velocity $v_1$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\Delta \\, \\text{KE} = \\dfrac{1}{2}mv_{1}^{2} - \\dfrac{1}{2}mv_{0}^{2}.\n$$\n\nWhen the mass and velocity are measured in kilograms and meters per second, respectively, the particle's kinetic energy is measured in Joules ($\\rm{J}$).\n\nFirst, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ v(t) & =\\int (4t+1)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ v(t) & =2{t}^{2}+t+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C,$ we use the fact that $v(0) = 0.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n0 = 2(0^2) + 0 + C\n\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\nC = 0.\n$$\n\nHence, the velocity of the particle at time $t$ is $v(t) = 2t^2 + t.$ In particular, its inital velocity at time $t = 1\\,\\rm{s}$ and final velocity at time $t = 2\\,\\rm{s}$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{0} & =v(1)=2({1}^{2})+1=3\\,m/s, \\\\ {v}_{1} & =v(2)=2({2}^{2})+2=10\\,m/s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the change in kinetic energy of the particle between these times is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ\\,\\text{KE} & =\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{1}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2\\cdot {10}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2\\cdot {3}^{2} \\\\  & =100-9 \\\\  & =91\\,J.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179440 :question/id #uuid "39eab788-1a8d-51af-b535-594cacc8ec2e" :question/math-academy-id "e-12527" :question/problem "A particle $P$ of mass $1\\,\\rm{kg}$ moves along the $x$-axis under the action of a resultant force. The displacement of the particle is given by $(t^2-2t+3)\\,\\rm{m},$ where $t \\geq 0$ is the time in seconds. Calculate the work done by the force between the times $t=1\\,\\rm{s}$ and $t=3\\,\\rm{s}.$" :question/worked-solution "The work-energy principle states that the work done by a resultant force on a particle equals the change in the particle's kinetic energy. That is,\n\n$$\nW\n= \n\\Delta\\, \\text{KE}\n$$\n\nwhere the change in kinetic energy of a particle is given by\n\n$$\n\\Delta\\, \\text{KE} = \\dfrac12 m v_1^2 - \\dfrac12 m v_0^2\n$$\n\nand $m$ is the particle's mass, $v_0$ is its initial velocity, and $v_1$ is its final velocity.\n\nThe displacement of the particle is given by $x(t)=t^2-2t+3,$ where $t$ is time in seconds.\nWe find the velocity $v(t)$ of the particle by differentiating the displacement with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}-2t+3) \\\\  & =2t-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the particle's initial velocity, at $t=1$, is\n\n$$\nv_0 = v(1) =  2(1) -2 = 0\\,\\rm{m/s},\n$$\n\nand its final velocity, at $t=3$, is\n\n$$\nv_1 = v(3) = 2(3) -2= 4\\,\\rm{m/s}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the work-energy principle, the work done by the force between these times is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W & =Δ\\,\\text{KE} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 1\\cdot (4)^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 1\\cdot {0}^{2} \\\\  & =8-0 \\\\  & =8\\,J.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179441 :question/id #uuid "8f79d7e9-377f-5012-a762-279839fc806a" :question/math-academy-id "e-12528" :question/problem "A particle $P$ of mass $2\\,\\rm{kg}$ moves along the $x$-axis under the action of a force of magnitude $(x+3)\\,\\rm{N}$ acting in the direction of increasing $x.$ Given that $x$ is measured in meters, and the particle has velocity $v=2\\,\\rm{m/s}$ at $x=2,$ calculate the velocity of the particle at $x=4.$" :question/worked-solution "The work-energy principle states that the work done by a resultant force on a particle equals the change in the particle's kinetic energy. That is,\n\n$$\nW\n= \n\\Delta\\, \\text{KE}\n$$\n\nwhere the change in kinetic energy of a particle is given by\n\n$$\n\\Delta\\, \\text{KE} = \\dfrac12 m v_1^2 - \\dfrac12 m v_0^2\n$$\n\nand $m$ is the particle's mass, $v_0$ is its initial velocity, and $v_1$ is its final velocity.\n\nFirst, we calculate the work done by the force in moving the particle from $x=2$ to $x=4{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W & ={\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}F(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{4}(x+3)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+3x]}_{2}^{4} \\\\  & =(8+12)-(2+6) \\\\  & =12\\,J\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe particle has velocity $v=2\\,\\rm{m/s}$ at $x=2.$ So, we can find the velocity $v_1$ at $x=4$ using the work-energy principle:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W & =\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{1}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{0}^{2} \\\\ 12 & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2\\cdot {v}_{1}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2\\cdot {2}^{2} \\\\ 12 & ={v}_{1}^{2}-4 \\\\ {v}_{1}^{2} & =16 \\\\ {v}_{1} & =\\pm 4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can disregard the negative solution since the velocity and acceleration of the particle are positive in the regions under consideration. \n\nTherefore, the velocity of the particle at $x=4$ is $4\\,\\rm{m/s}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180099 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "999c4d55-0cbb-5dcc-a421-563bcdceed78" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186178264} {:answer/id #uuid "0d2dfd7e-0b27-55db-809f-ca51f98cc0cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186178265} {:answer/id #uuid "43f22f78-4a1e-5622-9b3f-47a164b39748" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186178266} {:answer/id #uuid "5407afe9-51b6-5c08-a6e4-c5884596940d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186178267} {:answer/id #uuid "787f62d6-efa2-5ec5-ba5c-fec241cb9282" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186178268}] :answer-field/id #uuid "00d967dc-6c93-55d9-bc06-7bec8bf5cf28" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186178263} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ffd503b7-1fb0-5b6b-947d-f67bedec77bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389416} {:answer/id #uuid "923d3387-ad05-5dc6-b74d-db9f370d7b81" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389417} {:answer/id #uuid "5f2c785c-6a82-544b-9b53-c3267202ca39" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389418} {:answer/id #uuid "49129e10-9a8b-5b14-b52c-0e2c956cf3ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389419} {:answer/id #uuid "8129efb7-faee-5026-8faa-31e10d79448b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389420}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "49129e10-9a8b-5b14-b52c-0e2c956cf3ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389419} :answer-field/id #uuid "851383c4-adde-5677-a6f8-277d19fcdb87" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389415}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "8cf39bb1-9c63-5747-9e30-28aba079140a" :question/math-academy-id "q-163596" :question/problem "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis under the action of a force of magnitude $2{x}^{2}\\,\\text{N}$ acting in the direction of increasing $x.$ Given that $x$ is measured in meters, calculate the work done by the force in moving the particle from $x=0$ to $x=1.$" :question/worked-solution "The work done by a force $F=F(x)$ in moving a particle from $x={x}_{1}$ to $x={x}_{2}$ is given by\n\n$$\nW={\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}F(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWhen the force and distance are measured in Newtons and meters, respectively, the work done by the force is measured in Joules ($J$).\n\nSubstituting the given values into our integral, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}2{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}{x}^{3}{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}(1-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\,J.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180100 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3b968cb6-6ae9-5b0c-9af4-6dd1a21d39c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416155} {:answer/id #uuid "469e84d9-10e8-5bb8-8014-4bea0369b746" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416160} {:answer/id #uuid "6afd05e0-6e50-556e-93ad-2b191ada987b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416176} {:answer/id #uuid "8b758d59-3144-5ba6-9ee7-ab705a9dc58e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416189} {:answer/id #uuid "ebdc4bfd-1d77-5007-a2a4-775aba359b33" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416231}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3b968cb6-6ae9-5b0c-9af4-6dd1a21d39c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416155} :answer-field/id #uuid "188254e6-7bf6-52e7-85b7-577eb0a8ea66" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416239}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "856810c6-e294-5790-8bc1-67fd3e665a84" :question/math-academy-id "q-163597" :question/problem "A particle $P$ of mass $1.5\\,kg$ moves along the $x$-axis with an acceleration of magnitude $(2x+1)\\,{m/s}^{2}$ acting in the direction $OP.$ Given that $x$ is measured in meters, calculate the work done by the force in moving the particle from $x=1$ to $x=2.$" :question/worked-solution "The work done by a force $F=F(x)$ in moving a particle from $x={x}_{1}$ to $x={x}_{2}$ is given by\n\n$$\nW={\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}F(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWhen the force and distance are measured in Newtons and meters, respectively, the work done by the force is measured in Joules ($J$).\n\nUsing Newton's second law of motion, the force required to accelerate a particle of mass $1.5\\,kg$ at a rate of $(2x+1)\\,{m/s}^{2}$ is\n\n$$\nF=ma=1.5\\cdot (2x+1)=(3x+1.5)\\,N.\n$$\n\nNow, substituting the known values into our integral, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}(3x+1.5)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{2}+\\frac{3}{2}x]}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}({2}^{2}+2-({1}^{2}+1)) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}(6-2) \\\\  & =6\\,J.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180101 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c59d75c-f137-5ad0-adb1-d98444184b0b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416144} {:answer/id #uuid "112a9bb0-329a-50bc-862b-f1f1908e9ef2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416146} {:answer/id #uuid "832666e4-bfb7-5311-860f-b9c7edf6df68" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416186} {:answer/id #uuid "b8a7e4a3-4075-524e-b699-5f80dc6c213a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416210} {:answer/id #uuid "ef25fa57-c8c1-5068-8f4b-f19cebf60716" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416232}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "832666e4-bfb7-5311-860f-b9c7edf6df68" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416186} :answer-field/id #uuid "cb6dfeb2-3a92-53e8-a0f6-ee617bbb1e9c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416249}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "babebacc-c620-5336-84f2-4d7aa7dcc6d8" :question/math-academy-id "q-163598" :question/problem "A particle $P$ of mass $2\\,kg$ moves along the $x$-axis from rest at the origin with an acceleration of magnitude $2t\\,{m/s}^{2},$ where the acceleration acts in the direction of increasing $x,$ and $t\\ge 0$ is the time in seconds. Calculate the particle's kinetic energy at time $t=2\\,s.$" :question/worked-solution "The kinetic energy of a particle with mass $m$ and velocity $v$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{KE}=\\frac{1}{2}m{v}^{2}.\n$$\n\nWhen the mass and velocity are measured in kilograms and meters per second, respectively, the particle's kinetic energy is measured in Joules ($J$).\n\nFirst, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ v(t) & =\\int 2t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ v(t) & ={t}^{2}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C,$ we use the fact that $v(0)=0.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n0={0}^{2}+C\\,⟹\\,C=0.\n$$\n\nHence, the velocity of the particle at time $t$ is $v(t)={t}^{2}.$ In particular, its velocity at time $t=2\\,s$ is\n\n$$\nv=v(2)={2}^{2}=4\\,m/s.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the kinetic energy of the particle at $t=2$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{KE} & =\\frac{1}{2}m{v}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2\\cdot {4}^{2} \\\\  & =16\\,J.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180102 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a761dbb-e1d5-57df-9385-bd4910c225c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "128\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278691} {:answer/id #uuid "39f85c79-db0b-5926-b282-065e4cfe191a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "112\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279103} {:answer/id #uuid "452aeca0-b160-504e-9ba8-a4dada219d4e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "156\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279209} {:answer/id #uuid "82d5c9c6-112c-57da-8023-abc0f7cf3a49" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "132\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279763} {:answer/id #uuid "9b9b0cbb-434a-50cf-ac78-90cf76501c1b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "144\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279972}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9b9b0cbb-434a-50cf-ac78-90cf76501c1b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "144\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279972} :answer-field/id #uuid "faf8099c-21a2-5147-9426-e679ca2e1ba2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281280}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "b3b62346-21d6-5f32-ba80-7ff1aca6b5d6" :question/math-academy-id "q-163599" :question/problem "A particle $P$ of mass $3\\,kg$ moves along the $x$-axis from rest at the origin with an acceleration of magnitude $(3{t}^{2}+1)\\,{m/s}^{2},$ where the acceleration acts in the direction of increasing $x,$ and $t\\ge 0$ is the time in seconds. Calculate the change in kinetic energy of the particle between the times $t=1\\,s$ and $t=2\\,s.$" :question/worked-solution "The change in kinetic energy of a particle with mass $m,$ initial velocity ${v}_{0},$ and final velocity ${v}_{1}$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔ\\,\\text{KE}=\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{1}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{0}^{2}.\n$$\n\nWhen the mass and velocity are measured in kilograms and meters per second, respectively, the particle's kinetic energy is measured in Joules ($J$).\n\nFirst, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ v(t) & =\\int (3{t}^{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ v(t) & ={t}^{3}+t+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C,$ we use the fact that $v(0)=0.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n0={0}^{3}+0+C\\,⟹\\,C=0.\n$$\n\nHence, the velocity of the particle at time $t$ is $v(t)={t}^{3}+t.$ In particular, its inital velocity at time $t=1\\,s$ and final velocity at time $t=2\\,s$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{0} & =v(1)={1}^{3}+1=2\\,m/s, \\\\ {v}_{1} & =v(2)={2}^{3}+2=10\\,m/s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the change in kinetic energy of the particle between these times is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ\\,\\text{KE} & =\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{1}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 3\\cdot {10}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 3\\cdot {2}^{2} \\\\  & =150-6 \\\\  & =144\\,J.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180103 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "452dcb36-4e8e-5c4d-bbab-d59aa1427bbf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279210} {:answer/id #uuid "675b5d28-9c02-5ed8-b190-60068bfd7200" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279529} {:answer/id #uuid "7bcd8992-c86f-5ed8-8b85-3b006eb23206" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279696} {:answer/id #uuid "98b9878b-6e3c-5263-8d0a-a4133c54e84a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279941} {:answer/id #uuid "f3589965-bf1c-56f3-9f99-d2824d583527" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280718}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "98b9878b-6e3c-5263-8d0a-a4133c54e84a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279941} :answer-field/id #uuid "3768d6e3-78a3-5ed2-b58b-bc35eeb1c01e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280933}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "c92a85dc-fea5-582a-a4d0-c749606f94b3" :question/math-academy-id "q-163601" :question/problem "A particle $P$ of mass $2\\,kg$ moves along the $x$-axis under the action of a resultant force. The particle's velocity is $2t\\,m/s,$ where $t\\ge 0$ is the time in seconds. Calculate the work done by the force between the times $t=1\\,s$ and $t=2\\,s.$" :question/worked-solution "The work-energy principle states that the work done by a resultant force on a particle equals the change in the particle's kinetic energy. That is,\n\n$$\nW=Δ\\,\\text{KE}\n$$\n\nwhere the change in kinetic energy of a particle is given by\n\n$$\nΔ\\,\\text{KE}=\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{1}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{0}^{2}\n$$\n\nand $m$ is the particle's mass, ${v}_{0}$ is its initial velocity, and ${v}_{1}$ is its final velocity.\n\nThe particle's velocity is given by $v(t)=2t,$ where $t$ is time in seconds. So, the particle's initial velocity, at $t=1$, is\n\n$$\n{v}_{0}=v(1)=2(1)=2\\,m/s,\n$$\n\nand final velocity, at $t=2$, is\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=v(2)=2(2)=4\\,m/s.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the work-energy principle, the work done by the force between these times is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W & =Δ\\,\\text{KE} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2\\cdot (4)^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2\\cdot (2)^{2} \\\\  & =16-4 \\\\  & =12\\,J.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180105 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9085cdd3-9d32-5301-8cbf-ca557b3f00d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416192} {:answer/id #uuid "93b0a24f-9f97-5f8e-ae03-d64c89bef3e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "49\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416194} {:answer/id #uuid "c4de12ba-3316-5e1b-ac4d-ff0026c7af55" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "36\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416214} {:answer/id #uuid "c8dba406-896d-56df-ba18-d38eb5a20778" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "27\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416217} {:answer/id #uuid "fab72cab-a457-504c-bec5-6f9514a2cbb6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "25\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416236}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c4de12ba-3316-5e1b-ac4d-ff0026c7af55" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "36\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416214} :answer-field/id #uuid "26a75307-ee16-5df7-9ca9-2990c5ceb006" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416240}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "93920b26-fb04-52d5-8d58-1e17f6af55c8" :question/math-academy-id "q-163663" :question/problem "A particle $P$ of mass $3\\,kg$ moves along the $x$-axis under the action of a resultant force. The displacement of the particle is given by $({t}^{2}+t+1)\\,m,$ where $t\\ge 0$ is the time in seconds. Calculate the work done by the force between the times $t=0\\,s$ and $t=2\\,s.$" :question/worked-solution "The work-energy principle states that the work done by a resultant force on a particle equals the change in the particle's kinetic energy. That is,\n\n$$\nW=Δ\\,\\text{KE}\n$$\n\nwhere the change in kinetic energy of a particle is given by\n\n$$\nΔ\\,\\text{KE}=\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{1}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{0}^{2}\n$$\n\nand $m$ is the particle's mass, ${v}_{0}$ is its initial velocity, and ${v}_{1}$ is its final velocity.\n\nThe displacement of the particle is given by $x(t)={t}^{2}+t+1,$ where $t$ is time in seconds.\nWe find the velocity $v(t)$ of the particle by differentiating the displacement with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}+t+1) \\\\  & =2t+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the particle's initial velocity, at $t=0$, is\n\n$$\n{v}_{0}=v(0)=2(0)+1=1\\,m/s,\n$$\n\nand final velocity, at $t=2$, is\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=v(2)=2(2)+1=5\\,m/s.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the work-energy principle, the work done by the force between these times is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W & =Δ\\,\\text{KE} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 3\\cdot {5}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 3\\cdot {1}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{75}{2}-\\frac{3}{2} \\\\  & =36\\,J.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180106 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "11a230f6-8610-57b2-a192-bbc685af3d61" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{2}\\,m/s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416147} {:answer/id #uuid "2771a33d-a580-5640-b992-3faea8d28c68" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{2}\\,m/s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416151} {:answer/id #uuid "45f0d404-cb57-5e93-863a-e6e0439ae329" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\,m/s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416158} {:answer/id #uuid "b365bf0a-2cb2-5bc5-a6c1-23cd8ffbd67f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\,m/s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416205} {:answer/id #uuid "f5298386-5d77-55b8-8b1f-b593564733bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{16}{3}\\,m/s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416234}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b365bf0a-2cb2-5bc5-a6c1-23cd8ffbd67f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\,m/s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416205} :answer-field/id #uuid "ee421f31-693d-5e9b-9b13-f8f8930e49f0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416255}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "4f5f6801-1e10-5d42-880a-19ee72d417ff" :question/math-academy-id "q-163665" :question/problem "A particle $P$ of mass $2\\,kg$ moves along the $x$-axis under the action of a force of magnitude $6x\\,N$ acting in the direction of increasing $x.$ Given that $x$ is measured in meters, and the particle has velocity $v=4\\,m/s$ at $x=1,$ calculate the velocity of the particle at $x=2.$" :question/worked-solution "The work-energy principle states that the work done by a resultant force on a particle equals the change in the particle's kinetic energy. That is,\n\n$$\nW=Δ\\,\\text{KE}\n$$\n\nwhere the change in kinetic energy of a particle is given by\n\n$$\nΔ\\,\\text{KE}=\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{1}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{0}^{2}\n$$\n\nand $m$ is the particle's mass, ${v}_{0}$ is its initial velocity, and ${v}_{1}$ is its final velocity.\n\nFirst, we calculate the work done by the force in moving the particle from $x=1$ to $x=2:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W & ={\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}F(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}6x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3{x}^{2}{|}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =3(4-1) \\\\  & =9\\,J\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe particle has velocity $v=4\\,m/s$ at $x=1.$ So, we can find the velocity ${v}_{1}$ at $x=2$ using the work-energy principle:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W & =\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{1}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{0}^{2} \\\\ 9 & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2\\cdot {v}_{1}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2\\cdot {4}^{2} \\\\ 9 & ={v}_{1}^{2}-16 \\\\ {v}_{1}^{2} & =25 \\\\ {v}_{1} & =\\pm 5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can disregard the negative solution since the velocity and acceleration of the particle are positive in the regions under consideration. \n\nTherefore, the velocity of the particle at $x=2$ is $5\\,m/s.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180107 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5af78eb7-b0ac-5fb4-8298-83843c06cabd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{2}\\,m/s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416170} {:answer/id #uuid "7a400848-d283-509d-b2ce-69f7f8a4002f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\,m/s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416183} {:answer/id #uuid "86e20970-7c3b-5405-aba7-818b64498280" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{3}\\,m/s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416187} {:answer/id #uuid "c6037124-9ca8-59a2-a5ba-448ad5035b07" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\,m/s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416215} {:answer/id #uuid "c6e877fb-7b90-52aa-a3a8-3a656bf7b8f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\sqrt{2}\\,m/s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416216}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "86e20970-7c3b-5405-aba7-818b64498280" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{3}\\,m/s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416187} :answer-field/id #uuid "685ebe43-4362-5e9f-b513-b366e7e5ddc5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416244}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "6c1be8fb-89db-5bdf-8f42-bf7da0962b48" :question/math-academy-id "q-163667" :question/problem "A particle $P$ of mass $1\\,kg$ moves along the $x$-axis under the action of the force of magnitude $(3{x}^{2}+1)\\,N$ acting in the direction of increasing $x.$ Given that $x$ is measured in meters, the particle was initially at the origin, and the particle has velocity $v=4\\,m/s$ at $x=1,$ calculate the particle's initial velocity." :question/worked-solution "The work-energy principle states that the work done by a resultant force on a particle equals the change in the particle's kinetic energy. That is,\n\n$$\nW=Δ\\,\\text{KE}\n$$\n\nwhere the change in kinetic energy of a particle is given by\n\n$$\nΔ\\,\\text{KE}=\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{1}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{0}^{2}\n$$\n\nand $m$ is the particle's mass, ${v}_{0}$ is its initial velocity, and ${v}_{1}$ is its final velocity.\n\nFirst, we calculate the work done by the force in moving the particle from $x=0$ to $x=1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W & ={\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}F(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(3{x}^{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={x}^{3}+x{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =1+1-0 \\\\  & =2\\,J\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe particle has velocity $v=4\\,m/s$ at $x=1.$ So, we can find the velocity ${v}_{0}$ at $x=0$ using the work-energy principle:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W & =\\frac{1}{2}{m}_{1}{v}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{0}^{2} \\\\ 2 & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 1\\cdot {4}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 1\\cdot {v}_{0}^{2} \\\\ 2 & =8-\\frac{1}{2}{v}_{0}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}{v}_{0}^{2} & =6 \\\\ {v}_{0}^{2} & =12 \\\\ {v}_{0} & =\\pm 2\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can disregard the negative solution since the velocity and acceleration of the particle are positive in the regions under consideration. \n\nTherefore, the velocity of the particle at $x=0$ is $2\\sqrt{3}\\,m/s.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284400 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1e5a645f-5e3d-5b7f-ad0e-fa3f3d0b63a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "225\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281961} {:answer/id #uuid "47038e7e-3946-5f6a-899f-17ab13c5dd9e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "200\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282289} {:answer/id #uuid "5f480f0b-b931-57d9-ae2f-fc6cf90701bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "245\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282492} {:answer/id #uuid "69360eaf-cc6d-5f09-b857-3f15257d587a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "150\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282592} {:answer/id #uuid "962ab04d-7ced-542d-9393-548d2988f0ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "175\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282985}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1e5a645f-5e3d-5b7f-ad0e-fa3f3d0b63a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "225\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281961} :answer-field/id #uuid "0a715ac5-e6e0-56c1-a5af-84aa00803c1f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283952}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "4e950580-db1d-5816-a29c-edf1d82b46f6" :question/math-academy-id "q-163673" :question/problem "A particle $P$ of mass $1.5\\,kg$ moves along the $x$-axis under the action of a resultant force. The particle's velocity is $5t\\,m/s,$ where $t\\ge 0$ is the time in seconds. Calculate the work done by the force between the times $t=2\\,s$ and $t=4\\,s.$" :question/worked-solution "The work-energy principle states that the work done by a resultant force on a particle equals the change in the particle's kinetic energy. That is,\n\n$$\nW=Δ\\,\\text{KE}\n$$\n\nwhere the change in kinetic energy of a particle is given by\n\n$$\nΔ\\,\\text{KE}=\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{1}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{0}^{2}\n$$\n\nand $m$ is the particle's mass, ${v}_{0}$ is its initial velocity, and ${v}_{1}$ is its final velocity.\n\nThe particle's velocity is given by $v(t)=5t,$ where $t$ is time in seconds. So, the particle's initial velocity, at $t=2$, is\n\n$$\n{v}_{0}=v(2)=5(2)=10\\,m/s,\n$$\n\nand final velocity, at $t=4$, is\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=v(4)=5(4)=20\\,m/s.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the work-energy principle, the work done by the force between these times is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W & =Δ\\,\\text{KE} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 1.5\\cdot (20)^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 1.5\\cdot (10)^{2} \\\\  & =300-75 \\\\  & =225\\,J.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284401 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6a8fdcf6-e4e9-58bc-8a1f-f71669a57356" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "96\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282605} {:answer/id #uuid "7a64dcb2-4ddb-51e3-a775-45aa2b5bd380" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "144\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282737} {:answer/id #uuid "98595623-c2d4-50ec-a6bd-d29a009752b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "125\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283007} {:answer/id #uuid "c63a2679-585f-57d6-8e02-00f6c9560c3b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "81\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283414} {:answer/id #uuid "d279c5c7-8767-5df6-9266-01b32bc3c669" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "160\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283525}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7a64dcb2-4ddb-51e3-a775-45aa2b5bd380" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "144\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282737} :answer-field/id #uuid "063a643b-09bf-5702-9a22-690b86eee991" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283945}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "745ec234-6d22-591e-89d1-53df1375bd3c" :question/math-academy-id "q-163679" :question/problem "A particle $P$ of mass $6\\,kg$ moves along the $x$-axis under the action of a resultant force. The particle's acceleration is $(\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t}}+1)\\,{m/s}^{2},$ where $t\\ge 0$ is the time in seconds. If the particle has velocity $v=2\\,m/s$ when $t=0\\,s,$ calculate the work done by the force between the times $t=1\\,s$ and $t=4\\,s.$" :question/worked-solution "The work-energy principle states that the work done by a resultant force on a particle equals the change in the particle's kinetic energy. That is,\n\n$$\nW=Δ\\,\\text{KE}\n$$\n\nwhere the change in kinetic energy of a particle is given by\n\n$$\nΔ\\,\\text{KE}=\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{1}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{0}^{2}\n$$\n\nand $m$ is the particle's mass, ${v}_{0}$ is its initial velocity, and ${v}_{1}$ is its final velocity.\n\nThe acceleration of the particle is given by $a(t)=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t}}+1,$ where $t$ is time in seconds.\nWe find the velocity $v(t)$ of the particle by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t}}+1)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\sqrt{t}+t+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C,$ we use the fact that $v(0)=2.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n2=\\sqrt{0}+0+C\\,⟹\\,C=2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity of the particle at time $t$ is $v(t)=\\sqrt{t}+t+2.$\nIn particular, the particle's initial velocity, at $t=1,$ is\n\n$$\n{v}_{0}=v(1)=\\sqrt{1}+1+2=4\\,m/s,\n$$\n\nand final velocity, at $t=4$, is\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=v(4)=\\sqrt{4}+4+2=8\\,m/s.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the work-energy principle, the work done by the force between these times is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W & =Δ\\,\\text{KE} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 6\\cdot {8}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 6\\cdot {4}^{2} \\\\  & =192-48 \\\\  & =144\\,J.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284402 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2c59ab02-98d7-5392-bd01-7fe7417fcf03" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "65\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282081} {:answer/id #uuid "3339aeb4-434f-5e0f-867c-918203782954" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "58\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282148} {:answer/id #uuid "78d6e74d-1c0c-5fc8-aead-30a72958bb81" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "51\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282722} {:answer/id #uuid "97f6c360-16d7-53df-aca5-caeecf742ae1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "46\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283003} {:answer/id #uuid "a8753cd7-f0ae-5881-8198-9121d7715114" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "72\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283153}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2c59ab02-98d7-5392-bd01-7fe7417fcf03" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "65\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282081} :answer-field/id #uuid "55b1a70b-173c-5e95-ad82-ddcd9c006883" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284076}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "246a47ae-6c6b-5e78-8596-377d166e18fb" :question/math-academy-id "q-163680" :question/problem "A particle $P$ of mass $8\\,kg$ moves along the $x$-axis under the action of a resultant force. The particle's acceleration is $(2t-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}})\\,{m/s}^{2},$ where $t\\ge 1$ is the time in seconds. If the particle has velocity $v=2\\,m/s$ when $t=1\\,s,$ calculate the work done by the force between the times $t=1\\,s$ and $t=2\\,s.$" :question/worked-solution "The work-energy principle states that the work done by a resultant force on a particle equals the change in the particle's kinetic energy. That is,\n\n$$\nW=Δ\\,\\text{KE}\n$$\n\nwhere the change in kinetic energy of a particle is given by\n\n$$\nΔ\\,\\text{KE}=\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{1}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{0}^{2}\n$$\n\nand $m$ is the particle's mass, ${v}_{0}$ is its initial velocity, and ${v}_{1}$ is its final velocity.\n\nThe acceleration of the particle is given by $a(t)=2t-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}},$ where $t$ is time in seconds.\nWe find the velocity $v(t)$ of the particle by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (2t-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={t}^{2}+\\frac{1}{t}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C,$ we use the fact that $v(1)=2.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n2={1}^{2}+\\frac{1}{1}+C\\,⟹\\,C=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity of the particle at time $t$ is $v(t)={t}^{2}+\\frac{1}{t}.$\nIn particular, the particle's initial velocity, at $t=1,$ is\n\n$$\n{v}_{0}=v(1)=2\\,m/s,\n$$\n\nand final velocity, at $t=2,$ is\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=v(2)={2}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{9}{2}\\,m/s.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the work-energy principle, the work done by the force between these times is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W & =Δ\\,\\text{KE} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 8\\cdot {\\left(\\frac{9}{2}\\right)}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 8\\cdot {2}^{2} \\\\  & =81-16 \\\\  & =65\\,J.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284403 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4ee55d5c-a50d-5754-a61a-40836d3fca5c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{55}{6}\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282366} {:answer/id #uuid "71f8003e-06db-5a13-9091-014c75f0625c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282663} {:answer/id #uuid "b1be44a2-1627-5acd-96fb-07014d008851" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{21}{2}\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283226} {:answer/id #uuid "b1c30f5d-e4b5-5a32-b7f7-8a15728f56cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283227} {:answer/id #uuid "f46f943b-35a5-53cc-91ea-21096db9a4fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{17}{2}\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283825}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4ee55d5c-a50d-5754-a61a-40836d3fca5c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{55}{6}\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282366} :answer-field/id #uuid "7683b59a-6bab-5025-b9ff-79cdfdcd9ac8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284137}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "05a6a5d6-c744-5167-9600-f33679c2f372" :question/math-academy-id "q-163690" :question/problem "A particle $P$ of mass $2.5\\,kg$ moves along the $x$-axis with an acceleration of magnitude $(4-{x}^{2})\\,{m/s}^{2}$ acting in the direction $OP.$ Given that $x$ is measured in meters, calculate the work done by the force in moving the particle from $x=0$ to $x=1.$" :question/worked-solution "The work done by a force $F=F(x)$ in moving a particle from $x={x}_{1}$ to $x={x}_{2}$ is given by\n\n$$\nW={\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}F(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWhen the force and distance are measured in Newtons and meters, respectively, the work done by the force is measured in Joules ($J$).\n\nUsing Newton's second law of motion, the force required to accelerate a particle of mass $2.5\\,kg$ at a rate of $(4-{x}^{2})\\,{m/s}^{2}$ is\n\n$$\nF=ma=2.5\\cdot (4-{x}^{2})=(10-2.5{x}^{2})\\,N.\n$$\n\nNow, substituting the known values into our integral, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(10-2.5{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[10x-\\frac{5}{6}{x}^{3}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =(10(1)-\\frac{5}{6}({1}^{3}))-(10(0)-\\frac{5}{6}({0}^{3})) \\\\  & =10-\\frac{5}{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{55}{6}\\,J.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284404 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05bcfa63-e250-5772-b8fa-2c4e9e71f6b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8\\,m/s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281739} {:answer/id #uuid "15fec9ed-e81b-5ed7-867b-9f6ac727572f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\,m/s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281892} {:answer/id #uuid "2b4f6952-fbc2-5a72-87f6-73996eb9864d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\,m/s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282071} {:answer/id #uuid "370e8ea8-cc9e-5c45-9eb1-deeb98d74236" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12\\,m/s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282179} {:answer/id #uuid "495359a5-4480-57a2-94b2-a396b359d43a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\,m/s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282309}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "05bcfa63-e250-5772-b8fa-2c4e9e71f6b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8\\,m/s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281739} :answer-field/id #uuid "b5410973-0c14-56e3-adfe-3a886a243fe9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284251}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "887af6ae-7471-5993-b20c-5303fc29b7d3" :question/math-academy-id "q-163692" :question/problem "A particle $P$ of mass $1\\,kg$ moves along the $x$-axis under the action of a force of magnitude $6\\sqrt{x}\\,N$ acting in the direction of increasing $x.$ Given that $x$ is measured in meters, and the particle has velocity $v=2\\sqrt{2}\\,m/s$ at $x=1,$ calculate the velocity of the particle at $x=4.$" :question/worked-solution "The work-energy principle states that the work done by a resultant force on a particle equals the change in the particle's kinetic energy. That is,\n\n$$\nW=Δ\\,\\text{KE}\n$$\n\nwhere the change in kinetic energy of a particle is given by\n\n$$\nΔ\\,\\text{KE}=\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{1}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{0}^{2}\n$$\n\nand $m$ is the particle's mass, ${v}_{0}$ is its initial velocity, and ${v}_{1}$ is its final velocity.\n\nFirst, we calculate the work done by the force in moving the particle from $x=1$ to $x=4:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W & ={\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}F(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}6\\sqrt{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4{x}^{3/2}{|}_{1}^{4} \\\\  & =4(8-1) \\\\  & =28\\,J\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe particle has velocity $v=2\\sqrt{2}\\,m/s$ at $x=1.$ So, we can find the velocity ${v}_{1}$ at $x=4$ using the work-energy principle:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W & =\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{1}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{0}^{2} \\\\ 28 & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 1\\cdot {v}_{1}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 1\\cdot (2\\sqrt{2})^{2} \\\\ 28 & =\\frac{1}{2}{v}_{1}^{2}-4 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}{v}_{1}^{2} & =32 \\\\ {v}_{1}^{2} & =64 \\\\ {v}_{1} & =\\pm 8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can disregard the negative solution since the velocity and acceleration of the particle are positive in the regions under consideration. \n\nTherefore, the velocity of the particle at $x=4$ is $8\\,m/s.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284405 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "68817934-4f8c-5b25-982a-e8430c076132" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "({e}^{4}+3)\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282583} {:answer/id #uuid "bf90ec3a-205c-59ae-953f-b1073fa1de4c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4({e}^{4}+1)\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283356} {:answer/id #uuid "c42a8f0e-0806-5068-a74b-d77672788049" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{e}^{4}\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283400} {:answer/id #uuid "ccf20e51-4291-5b04-b75f-0daf610aa1f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2{e}^{4}+3)\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283480} {:answer/id #uuid "d8c9968a-70ba-551d-b45e-7fa1cda5947b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "({e}^{4}+1)\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283578}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "68817934-4f8c-5b25-982a-e8430c076132" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "({e}^{4}+3)\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282583} :answer-field/id #uuid "0c946bf5-0a2c-50c1-8d29-00567b0df552" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283958}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "a9be5f03-cd61-5d2c-b299-f09c540225cb" :question/math-academy-id "q-163693" :question/problem "A particle $P$ of mass $4\\,kg$ moves along the $x$-axis with an acceleration of magnitude $(\\frac{1}{x}+{x}^{3})\\,{m/s}^{2}$ acting in the direction $OP.$ Given that $x$ is measured in meters, calculate the work done by the force in moving the particle from $x=1$ to $x=e.$" :question/worked-solution "The work done by a force $F=F(x)$ in moving a particle from $x={x}_{1}$ to $x={x}_{2}$ is given by\n\n$$\nW={\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}F(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWhen the force and distance are measured in Newtons and meters, respectively, the work done by the force is measured in Joules ($J$).\n\nUsing Newton's second law of motion, the force required to accelerate a particle of mass $4\\,kg$ at a rate of $(\\frac{1}{x}+{x}^{3})\\,{m/s}^{2}$ is\n\n$$\nF=ma=4(\\frac{1}{x}+{x}^{3})=(\\frac{4}{x}+4{x}^{3})\\,N.\n$$\n\nNow, substituting the known values into our integral, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W & ={\\int }_{1}^{e}(\\frac{4}{x}+4{x}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[4\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+{x}^{4}]}_{1}^{e} \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{ln}⁡e+{e}^{4}-(4\\operatorname{ln}⁡1+{1}^{4}) \\\\  & =4+{e}^{4}-0-1 \\\\  & =({e}^{4}+3)\\,J.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284406 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "32a122d8-1fc3-5400-b58a-f25f700e6051" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{10}\\,m/s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282145} {:answer/id #uuid "357fb8aa-2735-5c27-82a6-2b73d1e9f497" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{5}\\,m/s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282164} {:answer/id #uuid "7da8ef42-332f-5b2c-8b6f-202321486ced" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{2}\\,m/s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282777} {:answer/id #uuid "9818d27b-09fa-5f87-8220-493f1e572c6e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{10}\\,m/s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283004} {:answer/id #uuid "de4523e2-6de3-5c88-a314-bca9450a756c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{5}\\,m/s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283630}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "32a122d8-1fc3-5400-b58a-f25f700e6051" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{10}\\,m/s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282145} :answer-field/id #uuid "2cc8971f-8cac-51b4-a4f7-e3960faa593e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284015}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "2b2c32af-8164-5f26-a216-6242d39e258f" :question/math-academy-id "q-163694" :question/problem "A particle $P$ of mass $6\\,kg$ moves along the $x$-axis under the action of a force of magnitude $(8-x)\\,N$ acting in the direction of increasing $x.$ Given that $x$ is measured in meters, and the particle has velocity $v=6\\,m/s$ at $x=1,$ calculate the velocity of the particle at $x=3.$" :question/worked-solution "The work-energy principle states that the work done by a resultant force on a particle equals the change in the particle's kinetic energy. That is,\n\n$$\nW=Δ\\,\\text{KE}\n$$\n\nwhere the change in kinetic energy of a particle is given by\n\n$$\nΔ\\,\\text{KE}=\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{1}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{0}^{2}\n$$\n\nand $m$ is the particle's mass, ${v}_{0}$ is its initial velocity, and ${v}_{1}$ is its final velocity.\n\nFirst, we calculate the work done by the force in moving the particle from $x=1$ to $x=3:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W & ={\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}F(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}(8-x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[8x-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}]}_{1}^{3} \\\\  & =(24-\\frac{9}{2})-(8-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =12\\,J\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe particle has velocity $v=6\\,m/s$ at $x=1.$ So, we can find the velocity ${v}_{1}$ at $x=3$ using the work-energy principle:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W & =\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{1}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{0}^{2} \\\\ 12 & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 6\\cdot {v}_{1}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 6\\cdot {6}^{2} \\\\ 12 & =3{v}_{1}^{2}-108 \\\\ 3{v}_{1} & =120 \\\\ {v}_{1}^{2} & =40 \\\\ {v}_{1} & =\\pm \\sqrt{40} \\\\ {v}_{1} & =\\pm 2\\sqrt{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can disregard the negative solution since the velocity and acceleration of the particle are positive in the regions under consideration. \n\nTherefore, the velocity of the particle at $x=3$ is $2\\sqrt{10}\\,m/s.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284407 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "00abd201-797b-5ac7-999e-86f0e16e21bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "32\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281694} {:answer/id #uuid "2007c0aa-e8c9-5c6d-9d85-f4bf1861a12b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281977} {:answer/id #uuid "4bb56bd4-5fac-50e4-a607-d72f20a87298" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282339} {:answer/id #uuid "5559bff4-c08e-5255-9372-ee99436f781f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "28\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282414} {:answer/id #uuid "fab198ba-ebfa-55e9-ac8a-90b546e7935c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283881}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5559bff4-c08e-5255-9372-ee99436f781f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "28\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282414} :answer-field/id #uuid "ec3fdf22-461c-5bd6-b6b3-7b9feb6158e5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284351}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "513d1bec-e219-5804-bfa6-de8da08bf29b" :question/math-academy-id "q-163695" :question/problem "A particle $P$ of mass $3.5\\,kg$ moves along the $x$-axis with an acceleration of magnitude $2{x}^{3}\\,{m/s}^{2}$ acting in the direction $OP.$ Given that $x$ is measured in meters, calculate the work done by the force in moving the particle from $x=0$ to $x=2.$" :question/worked-solution "The work done by a force $F=F(x)$ in moving a particle from $x={x}_{1}$ to $x={x}_{2}$ is given by\n\n$$\nW={\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}F(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWhen the force and distance are measured in Newtons and meters, respectively, the work done by the force is measured in Joules ($J$).\n\nUsing Newton's second law of motion, the force required to accelerate a particle of mass $3.5\\,kg$ at a rate of $2{x}^{3}\\,{m/s}^{2}$ is\n\n$$\nF=ma=3.5\\cdot 2{x}^{3}=7{x}^{3}\\,N.\n$$\n\nNow, substituting the known values into our integral, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}7{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{4}{x}^{4}\\,{|}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{4}(16-0) \\\\  & =28\\,J.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284408 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "12c7dfed-18c8-54f4-bcab-26c2276fbeb5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{15}\\,m/s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281868} {:answer/id #uuid "77113e2b-5fbd-53fd-b968-8026e03148bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}\\,m/s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282706} {:answer/id #uuid "c7ce7a4c-ffe3-5a79-85ff-456a7a0d37bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\sqrt{5}\\,m/s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283432} {:answer/id #uuid "d985863e-e07a-5779-8f38-065644e491f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\sqrt{3}\\,m/s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283584} {:answer/id #uuid "faec696b-d648-5a88-a2b2-00c545c58c5a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{15}\\,m/s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283884}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "77113e2b-5fbd-53fd-b968-8026e03148bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}\\,m/s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282706} :answer-field/id #uuid "7bf91504-47d7-52c1-933a-c816c0f8d4aa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284145}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "2947fb78-11ea-5025-a207-e9a07d4940f6" :question/math-academy-id "q-163696" :question/problem "A particle $P$ of mass $3\\,kg$ moves along the $x$-axis under the action of the force of magnitude $(\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}+x)\\,N$ acting in the direction of increasing $x.$ Given that $x$ is measured in meters, the particle was initially at $x=1,$ and the particle has velocity $v=3\\,m/s$ at $x=4,$ calculate the particle's initial velocity." :question/worked-solution "The work-energy principle states that the work done by a resultant force on a particle equals the change in the particle's kinetic energy. That is,\n\n$$\nW=Δ\\,\\text{KE}\n$$\n\nwhere the change in kinetic energy of a particle is given by\n\n$$\nΔ\\,\\text{KE}=\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{1}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{0}^{2}\n$$\n\nand $m$ is the particle's mass, ${v}_{0}$ is its initial velocity, and ${v}_{1}$ is its final velocity.\n\nFirst, we calculate the work done by the force in moving the particle from $x=1$ to $x=4:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W & ={\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}F(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}(\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}+x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}-\\frac{2}{x}){|}_{1}^{4} \\\\  & =(8-\\frac{1}{2})-(\\frac{1}{2}-2) \\\\  & =9\\,J\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe particle has velocity $v=3\\,m/s$ at $x=4.$ So, we can find the velocity ${v}_{0}$ at $x=1$ using the work-energy principle:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W & =\\frac{1}{2}{m}_{1}{v}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{0}^{2} \\\\ 9 & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 3\\cdot {3}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 3\\cdot {v}_{0}^{2} \\\\ 9 & =\\frac{27}{2}-\\frac{3}{2}{v}_{0}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{3}{2}{v}_{0}^{2} & =\\frac{9}{2} \\\\ {v}_{0}^{2} & =3 \\\\ {v}_{0} & =\\pm \\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can disregard the negative solution since the velocity and acceleration of the particle are positive in the regions under consideration. \n\nTherefore, the velocity of the particle at $x=1$ is $\\sqrt{3}\\,m/s.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284409 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "17801320-b5c2-55ee-82c1-af48291b1ce3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{7}\\,m/s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281904} {:answer/id #uuid "194807a2-4bfd-5734-9b77-983343b518cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8\\,m/s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281918} {:answer/id #uuid "38d1d6e1-56b4-50eb-ba2a-f1ee7fa62b09" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\sqrt{7}\\,m/s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282187} {:answer/id #uuid "92a19260-2c33-5e1b-a35b-2c913fc35227" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\,m/s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282953} {:answer/id #uuid "9c55c79e-d08c-51f4-a43c-87e11fb875ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\,m/s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283051}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "92a19260-2c33-5e1b-a35b-2c913fc35227" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\,m/s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282953} :answer-field/id #uuid "91195aa8-3f41-5732-9fb8-745fffeba81a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284183}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "0bcc3c8d-345a-552c-94eb-d22373d2ea7f" :question/math-academy-id "q-163698" :question/problem "A particle $P$ of mass $7\\,kg$ moves along the $x$-axis under the action of the force of magnitude $(2x+3)\\,N$ acting in the direction of increasing $x.$ Given that $x$ is measured in meters, the particle was initially at $x=1,$ and the particle has velocity $v=2\\sqrt{5}\\,m/s$ at $x=3,$ calculate the particle's initial velocity." :question/worked-solution "The work-energy principle states that the work done by a resultant force on a particle equals the change in the particle's kinetic energy. That is,\n\n$$\nW=Δ\\,\\text{KE}\n$$\n\nwhere the change in kinetic energy of a particle is given by\n\n$$\nΔ\\,\\text{KE}=\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{1}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{0}^{2}\n$$\n\nand $m$ is the particle's mass, ${v}_{0}$ is its initial velocity, and ${v}_{1}$ is its final velocity.\n\nFirst, we calculate the work done by the force in moving the particle from $x=1$ to $x=3:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W & ={\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}F(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}(2x+3)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =({x}^{2}+3x){|}_{1}^{3} \\\\  & =(9+9)-(1+3) \\\\  & =14\\,J\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe particle has velocity $v=2\\sqrt{5}\\,m/s$ at $x=3.$ So, we can find the velocity ${v}_{0}$ at $x=1$ using the work-energy principle:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W & =\\frac{1}{2}{m}_{1}{v}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{0}^{2} \\\\ 14 & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 7\\cdot (2\\sqrt{5})^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 7\\cdot {v}_{0}^{2} \\\\ 14 & =70-\\frac{7}{2}{v}_{0}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{7}{2}{v}_{0}^{2} & =56 \\\\ {v}_{0}^{2} & =16 \\\\ {v}_{0} & =\\pm 4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can disregard the negative solution since the velocity and acceleration of the particle are positive in the regions under consideration. \n\nTherefore, the velocity of the particle at $x=1$ is $4\\,m/s.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284410 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0eda2ad3-47af-5be7-b608-001f5867a070" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8\\,m/s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281822} {:answer/id #uuid "3b5fc092-e01e-5e80-b17b-fa211816862a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\,m/s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282210} {:answer/id #uuid "7ad9a385-b7bd-5d55-8fe7-db61d449b0c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7\\,m/s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282743} {:answer/id #uuid "98dea36a-30a1-51a9-bec0-a63179316f21" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\,m/s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283015} {:answer/id #uuid "df25cf76-50ce-5bc6-9473-3b176dae4e10" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\,m/s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283636}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "98dea36a-30a1-51a9-bec0-a63179316f21" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\,m/s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283015} :answer-field/id #uuid "4bc66297-4a51-526d-9c85-6730aee7742c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284060}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "fa077697-3e40-5b75-b12d-524833765c75" :question/math-academy-id "q-163700" :question/problem "A particle $P$ of mass $4\\,kg$ moves along the $x$-axis under the action of the force of magnitude $({x}^{3}-4)\\,N$ acting in the direction of increasing $x.$ Given that $x$ is measured in meters, the particle was initially at $x=3$, and the particle has velocity $v=10\\,m/s$ at $x=5,$ calculate the particle's initial velocity." :question/worked-solution "The work-energy principle states that the work done by a resultant force on a particle equals the change in the particle's kinetic energy. That is,\n\n$$\nW=Δ\\,\\text{KE}\n$$\n\nwhere the change in kinetic energy of a particle is given by\n\n$$\nΔ\\,\\text{KE}=\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{1}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{0}^{2}\n$$\n\nand $m$ is the particle's mass, ${v}_{0}$ is its initial velocity, and ${v}_{1}$ is its final velocity.\n\nFirst, we calculate the work done by the force in moving the particle from $x=3$ to $x=5:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W & ={\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}F(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{3}^{5}({x}^{3}-4)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =(\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4}-4x){|}_{3}^{5} \\\\  & =(\\frac{625}{4}-20)-(\\frac{81}{4}-12) \\\\  & =128\\,J\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe particle has velocity $v=10\\,m/s$ at $x=5.$ So, we can find the velocity ${v}_{0}$ at $x=3$ using the work-energy principle:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W & =\\frac{1}{2}{m}_{1}{v}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{0}^{2} \\\\ 128 & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 4\\cdot {10}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 4\\cdot {v}_{0}^{2} \\\\ 128 & =200-2{v}_{0}^{2} \\\\ 2{v}_{0}^{2} & =72 \\\\ {v}_{0}^{2} & =36 \\\\ {v}_{0} & =\\pm 6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can disregard the negative solution since the velocity and acceleration of the particle are positive in the regions under consideration. \n\nTherefore, the velocity of the particle at $x=3$ is $6\\,m/s.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284411 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e7b0e40-333a-5bc8-bcc7-d89a11727067" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\,m/s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281814} {:answer/id #uuid "1804f732-7901-5b9a-ba35-4561860f8862" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1\\,m/s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281905} {:answer/id #uuid "bf7c6619-9d1d-5aa2-abaf-0cb2f52689cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{5}\\,m/s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283355} {:answer/id #uuid "d1436aef-27b7-53bf-a04b-a6da92fd05bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}\\,m/s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283514} {:answer/id #uuid "d85d791f-d408-5d46-a3bb-3cea1a65fc18" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\,m/s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283575}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1804f732-7901-5b9a-ba35-4561860f8862" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1\\,m/s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281905} :answer-field/id #uuid "dddf0264-d4ac-56c7-b08a-98cc45e03c62" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284322}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "7a3690d2-58b3-577e-ab63-76513fea5351" :question/math-academy-id "q-163701" :question/problem "A particle $P$ of mass $5\\,kg$ moves along the $x$-axis under the action of the force of magnitude $3x\\,N$ acting in the direction of increasing $x.$ Given that distances along the $x$-axis are measured in meters, the particle is initially at $x=1,$ and the particle has velocity $v=5\\,m/s$ at $x=4,$ find the particle's change in velocity in moving from $x=1$ to $x=4.$" :question/worked-solution "The work-energy principle states that the work done by a resultant force on a particle equals the change in the particle's kinetic energy. That is,\n\n$$\nW=Δ\\,\\text{KE}\n$$\n\nwhere the change in kinetic energy of a particle is given by\n\n$$\nΔ\\,\\text{KE}=\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{1}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{0}^{2}\n$$\n\nand $m$ is the particle's mass, ${v}_{0}$ is its initial velocity, and ${v}_{1}$ is its final velocity.\n\nFirst, we calculate the work done by the force in moving the particle from $x=1$ to $x=4:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W & ={\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}F(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}3x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{2}]}_{1}^{4} \\\\  & =24-\\frac{3}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{45}{2}\\,J\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe particle has velocity $v=5\\,m/s$ at $x=4.$ So, we can find the velocity ${v}_{0}$ at $x=1$ using the work-energy principle:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W & =\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{1}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{0}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{45}{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 5\\cdot {5}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 5\\cdot {v}_{0}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{45}{2} & =\\frac{125}{2}-\\frac{5}{2}{v}_{0}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{5}{2}{v}_{0}^{2} & =40 \\\\ {v}_{0}^{2} & =16 \\\\ {v}_{0} & =\\pm 4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can disregard the negative solution since the velocity and acceleration of the particle are positive in the regions under consideration. \n\nTherefore, the velocity of the particle at $x=1$ is $4\\,m/s.$\n\nFinally, the particle's change in velocity from $x=1$ to $x=4$ is\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}-{v}_{0}=5-4=1\\,m/s.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284412 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "21ed1623-8cd8-5607-b7ee-667f90f997f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "36\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281989} {:answer/id #uuid "513a8606-0827-5e85-8d13-ed68e7da06d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282386} {:answer/id #uuid "94c151a7-ac7e-5688-91e4-a47a42be9037" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "32\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282970} {:answer/id #uuid "9ce04f73-ff1e-5e68-a184-7c06ef4b36f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283057} {:answer/id #uuid "acd94cc5-0451-535e-881e-39a5faf635aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283186}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "94c151a7-ac7e-5688-91e4-a47a42be9037" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "32\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282970} :answer-field/id #uuid "709a12d6-e456-50d6-ada3-5e823367aecb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284123}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "0e70ff7a-49b4-581a-9f27-b1d83af80fad" :question/math-academy-id "q-163705" :question/problem "A particle $P$ of mass $4\\,kg$ moves along the $x$-axis from rest at the origin with an acceleration of magnitude ${t}^{3}\\,{m/s}^{2},$ where the acceleration acts in the direction of increasing $x,$ and $t\\ge 0$ is the time in seconds. Calculate the particle's kinetic energy at time $t=2\\,s.$" :question/worked-solution "The kinetic energy of a particle with mass $m$ and velocity $v$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{KE}=\\frac{1}{2}m{v}^{2}.\n$$\n\nWhen the mass and velocity are measured in kilograms and meters per second, respectively, the particle's kinetic energy is measured in Joules ($J$).\n\nFirst, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ v(t) & =\\int {t}^{3}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ v(t) & =\\frac{1}{4}{t}^{4}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C,$ we use the fact that $v(0)=0.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n0=\\frac{1}{4}(0)^{4}+C\\,⟹\\,C=0.\n$$\n\nHence, the velocity of the particle at time $t$ is $v(t)=\\frac{1}{4}{t}^{4}.$ In particular, its velocity at time $t=2\\,s$ is\n\n$$\nv=v(2)=\\frac{1}{4}({2}^{4})=4\\,m/s.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the kinetic energy of the particle at $t=2$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{KE} & =\\frac{1}{2}m{v}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 4\\cdot {4}^{2} \\\\  & =32\\,J.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284413 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "35c248d8-6b31-55df-90dc-c38dca6d9d1b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{16}{3}\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282166} {:answer/id #uuid "8204ddb8-cadf-55f1-8f7e-df954cc1809b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{15}{2}\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282806} {:answer/id #uuid "d61dbd69-68bc-5e04-913d-48cdcc5d5273" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{3}\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283551} {:answer/id #uuid "ecbcffb2-3c48-5a44-baa0-c1276cbbb8cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{2}\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283760} {:answer/id #uuid "fed9d1cf-f210-5139-bc5c-11052ee89353" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{6}\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283926}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "35c248d8-6b31-55df-90dc-c38dca6d9d1b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{16}{3}\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282166} :answer-field/id #uuid "6180ffa8-3a86-5267-96ab-9659265441d9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284093}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "9ae927bc-bcdc-5e2c-8e7d-9c28c8a41e0e" :question/math-academy-id "q-163708" :question/problem "A particle $P$ of mass $6\\,kg$ moves along the $x$-axis from rest at the origin with an acceleration of magnitude $(1+{t}^{2})\\,{m/s}^{2},$ where the acceleration acts in the direction of increasing $x,$ and $t\\ge 0$ is the time in seconds. Calculate the particle's kinetic energy at time $t=1\\,s.$" :question/worked-solution "The kinetic energy of a particle with mass $m$ and velocity $v$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{KE}=\\frac{1}{2}m{v}^{2}.\n$$\n\nWhen the mass and velocity are measured in kilograms and meters per second, respectively, the particle's kinetic energy is measured in Joules ($J$).\n\nFirst, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ v(t) & =\\int (1+{t}^{2})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ v(t) & =t+\\frac{1}{3}{t}^{3}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C,$ we use the fact that $v(0)=0.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n0=0+\\frac{1}{3}({0}^{3})+C\\,⟹\\,C=0.\n$$\n\nHence, the velocity of the particle at time $t$ is $v(t)=t+\\frac{1}{3}{t}^{3}.$ In particular, its velocity at time $t=1\\,s$ is\n\n$$\nv=v(1)=1+\\frac{1}{3}({1}^{3})=\\frac{4}{3}\\,m/s.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the kinetic energy of the particle at $t=1$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{KE} & =\\frac{1}{2}m{v}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 6\\cdot {\\left(\\frac{4}{3}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 6\\cdot \\frac{16}{9} \\\\  & =\\frac{16}{3}\\,J.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284414 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3566336a-5888-5f34-9857-34a10821debc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282163} {:answer/id #uuid "3e412ad6-1794-570f-ae80-6d4f0856759e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{4}\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282231} {:answer/id #uuid "856b7b4b-2869-583d-992c-856b97da7887" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282841} {:answer/id #uuid "acdefad4-062f-5397-94c0-1e20952ffdb9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283187} {:answer/id #uuid "bc9a8ad4-ea15-5b73-937e-1a97b80f1cfd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283324}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3e412ad6-1794-570f-ae80-6d4f0856759e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{4}\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282231} :answer-field/id #uuid "d0639efc-1c73-54b7-b668-9bbd9efdc629" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284298}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "a709c2cf-ccd1-5398-9c64-aedbfd0bbb7d" :question/math-academy-id "q-163712" :question/problem "A particle $P$ of mass $2\\,kg$ moves along the $x$-axis from rest at the origin with an acceleration of magnitude $2{e}^{-t}\\,{m/s}^{2},$ where the acceleration acts in the direction of increasing $x,$ and $t\\ge 0$ is the time in seconds. Calculate the change in kinetic energy of the particle between the times $t=\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\,s$ and $t=\\operatorname{ln}⁡4\\,s.$" :question/worked-solution "The change in kinetic energy of a particle with mass $m,$ initial velocity ${v}_{0},$ and final velocity ${v}_{1}$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔ\\,\\text{KE}=\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{1}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{0}^{2}.\n$$\n\nWhen the mass and velocity are measured in kilograms and meters per second, respectively, the particle's kinetic energy is measured in Joules ($J$).\n\nFirst, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ v(t) & =\\int 2{e}^{-t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ v(t) & =-2{e}^{-t}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C,$ we use the fact that $v(0)=0.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n0=-2{e}^{-0}+C\\,⟹\\,C=2.\n$$\n\nHence, the velocity of the particle at time $t$ is $v(t)=2-2{e}^{-t}.$ In particular, its inital velocity at time $t=\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\,s$ and final velocity at time $t=\\operatorname{ln}⁡4\\,s$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{0} & =v(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)=2-2{e}^{-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}=1\\,m/s, \\\\ {v}_{1} & =v(\\operatorname{ln}⁡4)=2-2{e}^{-\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}=\\frac{3}{2}\\,m/s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the change in kinetic energy of the particle between these times is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ\\,\\text{KE} & =\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{1}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2\\cdot {\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2\\cdot {(1)}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{4}-1 \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{4}\\,J.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186376131 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cc1a8e88-caed-5cb8-b087-8aa21552c9bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "132\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376133} {:answer/id #uuid "98965c6f-696f-5b4b-a818-d4ee3eed08ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "64\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376134} {:answer/id #uuid "f124a2ef-b71c-5f40-b426-4271c9bbfd72" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "78\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376135} {:answer/id #uuid "7ccabb96-6694-5d14-8af3-a3f18d64a23e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "96\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376136} {:answer/id #uuid "83ee8dfa-ac8a-5f7b-b0f7-d8c07168808d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "128\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376137}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "83ee8dfa-ac8a-5f7b-b0f7-d8c07168808d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "128\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376137} :answer-field/id #uuid "abf10214-129c-5273-9a15-d862fef6c64e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376132}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "2946ea8d-fc5d-5e1c-a7ad-b8ebee7d21a3" :question/math-academy-id "q-163675" :question/problem "A particle $P$ of mass $4\\,kg$ moves along the $x$-axis under the action of a resultant force. The displacement of the particle is given by $(2{t}^{3}+{t}^{2})\\,m,$ where $t\\ge 0$ is the time in seconds. Calculate the work done by the force between the times $t=0\\,s$ and $t=1\\,s.$" :question/worked-solution "The work-energy principle states that the work done by a resultant force on a particle equals the change in the particle's kinetic energy. That is,\n\n$$\nW=Δ\\,\\text{KE}\n$$\n\nwhere the change in kinetic energy of a particle is given by\n\n$$\nΔ\\,\\text{KE}=\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{1}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{0}^{2}\n$$\n\nand $m$ is the particle's mass, ${v}_{0}$ is its initial velocity, and ${v}_{1}$ is its final velocity.\n\nThe displacement of the particle is given by $x(t)=2{t}^{3}+{t}^{2},$ where $t$ is time in seconds.\nWe find the velocity $v(t)$ of the particle by differentiating the displacement with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{t}^{3}+{t}^{2}) \\\\  & =6{t}^{2}+2t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the particle's initial velocity, at $t=0$, is\n\n$$\n{v}_{0}=v(0)=6(0)^{2}+2(0)=0\\,m/s,\n$$\n\nand final velocity, at $t=1$, is\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=v(1)=6(1)^{2}+2(1)=8\\,m/s.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the work-energy principle, the work done by the force between these times is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W & =Δ\\,\\text{KE} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 4\\cdot {8}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 4\\cdot {0}^{2} \\\\  & =128-0 \\\\  & =128\\,J.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389428 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0882e3f7-f97e-5d5b-ab30-55393a6df5f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "246\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389430} {:answer/id #uuid "7ce75a48-a50b-51ed-9b2e-3bd4b6c7cf4a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "266\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389431} {:answer/id #uuid "5f93d06c-8297-5f19-a7a9-1e8d58499e54" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "236\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389432} {:answer/id #uuid "11eaead2-18c3-507d-ad5c-e81f26231b38" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "226\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389433} {:answer/id #uuid "3c90e325-3192-5d5b-985f-014dab1a5254" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "256\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389434}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7ce75a48-a50b-51ed-9b2e-3bd4b6c7cf4a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "266\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389431} :answer-field/id #uuid "b16601f4-df28-5d36-9cea-33a8a93f0535" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389429}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "e3727b71-f77c-52d3-b736-d8fbf4a3880a" :question/math-academy-id "q-163659" :question/problem "A particle $P$ of mass $4\\,kg$ moves along the $x$-axis under the action of a resultant force. The particle's acceleration is $(4t+1)\\,{m/s}^{2},$ where $t\\ge 0$ is the time in seconds. If the particle has velocity $v=3\\,m/s$ when $t=0\\,s,$ calculate the work done by the force between the times $t=1\\,s$ and $t=2\\,s.$" :question/worked-solution "The work-energy principle states that the work done by a resultant force on a particle equals the change in the particle's kinetic energy. That is,\n\n$$\nW=Δ\\,\\text{KE}\n$$\n\nwhere the change in kinetic energy of a particle is given by\n\n$$\nΔ\\,\\text{KE}=\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{1}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{0}^{2}\n$$\n\nand $m$ is the particle's mass, ${v}_{0}$ is its initial velocity, and ${v}_{1}$ is its final velocity.\n\nThe acceleration of the particle is given by $a(t)=4t+1,$ where $t$ is time in seconds.\nWe find the velocity $v(t)$ of the particle by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (4t+1)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2{t}^{2}+t+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C,$ we use the fact that $v(0)=3.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n3=2(0)^{2}+0+C\\,⟹\\,C=3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity of the particle at time $t$ is $v(t)=2{t}^{2}+t+3.$\nIn particular, the particle's initial velocity, at $t=1$, is\n\n$$\n{v}_{0}=v(1)=2(1)^{2}+1+3=6\\,m/s,\n$$\n\nand final velocity, at $t=2$, is\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=v(2)=2(2)^{2}+2+3=13\\,m/s.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the work-energy principle, the work done by the force between these times is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W & =Δ\\,\\text{KE} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 4\\cdot {13}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 4\\cdot {6}^{2} \\\\  & =338-72 \\\\  & =266\\,J.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389435 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "70f35aeb-2053-5b37-abce-48d3972b397c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "25\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389437} {:answer/id #uuid "24f06dbe-e866-519e-8599-01c0ead2742e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389438} {:answer/id #uuid "7a155385-200c-550f-96bf-08d5a75d20fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389439} {:answer/id #uuid "318dfa74-61f2-571e-8498-0ef0bc26ce59" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "17\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389440} {:answer/id #uuid "156b8685-b780-52be-a010-0b4b0806771e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389441}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "156b8685-b780-52be-a010-0b4b0806771e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389441} :answer-field/id #uuid "c13537c9-cba9-528c-81df-ec823b958306" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389436}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "8305d2ef-8fb9-5f80-b2fe-3b5b2380f4bf" :question/math-academy-id "q-163671" :question/problem "A particle $P$ of mass $1\\,kg$ moves along the $x$-axis under the action of a resultant force. The particle's velocity is $(t+1)\\,m/s,$ where $t\\ge 0$ is the time in seconds. Calculate the work done by the force between the times $t=2\\,s$ and $t=4\\,s.$" :question/worked-solution "The work-energy principle states that the work done by a resultant force on a particle equals the change in the particle's kinetic energy. That is,\n\n$$\nW=Δ\\,\\text{KE}\n$$\n\nwhere the change in kinetic energy of a particle is given by\n\n$$\nΔ\\,\\text{KE}=\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{1}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{0}^{2}\n$$\n\nand $m$ is the particle's mass, ${v}_{0}$ is its initial velocity, and ${v}_{1}$ is its final velocity.\n\nThe particle's velocity is given by $v(t)=t+1,$ where $t$ is time in seconds. So, the particle's initial velocity, at $t=2$, is\n\n$$\n{v}_{0}=v(2)=2+1=3\\,m/s,\n$$\n\nand final velocity, at $t=4$, is\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=v(4)=4+1=5\\,m/s.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the work-energy principle, the work done by the force between these times is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W & =Δ\\,\\text{KE} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 1\\cdot (5)^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 1\\cdot (3)^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{25}{2}-\\frac{9}{2} \\\\  & =8\\,J.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389442 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "56559d07-36b9-5fba-b51e-1f8e5cba51e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "253\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389444} {:answer/id #uuid "c613f699-7516-5c10-a37c-3efc4c225fe9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "275\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389445} {:answer/id #uuid "796b1a5b-ed2a-5e7a-b39a-e70d3438bf86" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "242\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389446} {:answer/id #uuid "9f89a0c0-4a4c-5eeb-833d-bfc360dbff90" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "264\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389447} {:answer/id #uuid "66326f34-27cb-58c3-9945-4137e4710b64" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "280\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389448}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "56559d07-36b9-5fba-b51e-1f8e5cba51e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "253\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389444} :answer-field/id #uuid "3b3b8e1e-c87a-5182-99ea-c5567dd2e35b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389443}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "f249579b-a99f-5b51-b0c7-e7f72d9a4512" :question/math-academy-id "q-163678" :question/problem "A particle $P$ of mass $2\\,kg$ moves along the $x$-axis under the action of a resultant force. The particle's acceleration is $(3{t}^{2}+4)\\,{m/s}^{2},$ where $t\\ge 0$ is the time in seconds. If the particle has velocity $v=1\\,m/s$ when $t=0\\,s,$ calculate the work done by the force between the times $t=1\\,s$ and $t=2\\,s.$" :question/worked-solution "The work-energy principle states that the work done by a resultant force on a particle equals the change in the particle's kinetic energy. That is,\n\n$$\nW=Δ\\,\\text{KE}\n$$\n\nwhere the change in kinetic energy of a particle is given by\n\n$$\nΔ\\,\\text{KE}=\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{1}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{0}^{2}\n$$\n\nand $m$ is the particle's mass, ${v}_{0}$ is its initial velocity, and ${v}_{1}$ is its final velocity.\n\nThe acceleration of the particle is given by $a(t)=3{t}^{2}+4,$ where $t$ is time in seconds.\nWe find the velocity $v(t)$ of the particle by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (3{t}^{2}+4)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={t}^{3}+4t+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C,$ we use the fact that $v(0)=1.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n1=(0)^{3}+4(0)+C\\,⟹\\,C=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity of the particle at time $t$ is $v(t)={t}^{3}+4t+1.$\nIn particular, the particle's initial velocity, at $t=1,$ is\n\n$$\n{v}_{0}=v(1)=(1)^{3}+4(1)+1=6\\,m/s,\n$$\n\nand final velocity, at $t=2,$ is\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=v(2)=(2)^{3}+4(2)+1=17\\,m/s.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the work-energy principle, the work done by the force between these times is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W & =Δ\\,\\text{KE} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2\\cdot {17}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2\\cdot {6}^{2} \\\\  & =289-36 \\\\  & =253\\,J.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389449 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "eb4d826c-14ba-585c-aebd-1facb3368012" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389451} {:answer/id #uuid "8ab762ed-0112-5d52-b62f-05b3b484b0b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389452} {:answer/id #uuid "6aa053a0-465d-5f0b-8742-c3a588a86e3e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389453} {:answer/id #uuid "ee6add72-368a-56d1-a701-8e8fbe0ceee5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389454} {:answer/id #uuid "04400f5c-b01f-5a74-aa41-074c903d6463" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389455}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "eb4d826c-14ba-585c-aebd-1facb3368012" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389451} :answer-field/id #uuid "7fc72668-2f1d-508f-943f-15caead3623c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389450}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "d31a00a5-c404-50d8-b7f2-fda34cf2cc53" :question/math-academy-id "q-163685" :question/problem "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis under the action of a force of magnitude $\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{N}$ acting in the direction of increasing $x.$ Given that $x$ is measured in meters, calculate the work done by the force in moving the particle from $x=1$ to $x=4.$" :question/worked-solution "The work done by a force $F=F(x)$ in moving a particle from $x={x}_{1}$ to $x={x}_{2}$ is given by\n\n$$\nW={\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}F(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWhen the force and distance are measured in Newtons and meters, respectively, the work done by the force is measured in Joules ($J$).\n\nSubstituting the given values into our integral, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{x}\\,{|}_{1}^{4} \\\\  & =-(\\frac{1}{4}-1) \\\\  & =-\\left(-\\frac{3}{4}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}\\,J.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389456 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c199cc2a-f7f6-59a6-a5fc-4846aee05fdc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\,m/s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389458} {:answer/id #uuid "442ec423-11ee-5f69-93dc-a03ed507c7da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\,m/s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389459} {:answer/id #uuid "68180a5e-f1ad-55a4-b5d8-b911509b32e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{4}\\,m/s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389460} {:answer/id #uuid "7d542d69-ec75-5271-91be-39c1464d14a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}\\,m/s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389461} {:answer/id #uuid "6fde94bd-e7bd-5cbc-a4de-8543a3385c56" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\,m/s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389462}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c199cc2a-f7f6-59a6-a5fc-4846aee05fdc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\,m/s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389458} :answer-field/id #uuid "a040d79b-c119-5a8f-9a95-eb5d0defe231" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389457}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "89e50146-b1c6-571f-ad82-cc9a4f7f02cf" :question/math-academy-id "q-163702" :question/problem "A particle $P$ of mass $1\\,kg$ moves along the $x$-axis under the action of the force of magnitude $(3{x}^{2}+2)\\,N$ acting in the direction of increasing $x.$ Given that distances along the $x$-axis are measured in meters, the particle is initially at the origin, and the particle has velocity $v=7\\,m/s$ at $x=2,$ find the particle's change in velocity in moving from $x=0$ to $x=2.$" :question/worked-solution "The work-energy principle states that the work done by a resultant force on a particle equals the change in the particle's kinetic energy. That is,\n\n$$\nW=Δ\\,\\text{KE}\n$$\n\nwhere the change in kinetic energy of a particle is given by\n\n$$\nΔ\\,\\text{KE}=\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{1}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{0}^{2}\n$$\n\nand $m$ is the particle's mass, ${v}_{0}$ is its initial velocity, and ${v}_{1}$ is its final velocity.\n\nFirst, we calculate the work done by the force in moving the particle from $x=0$ to $x=2:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W & ={\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}F(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(3{x}^{2}+2)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[{x}^{3}+2x]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =8+4-0 \\\\  & =12\\,J\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe particle has velocity $v=7\\,m/s$ at $x=2.$ So, we can find the velocity ${v}_{0}$ at $x=0$ using the work-energy principle:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W & =\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{1}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{0}^{2} \\\\ 12 & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 1\\cdot {7}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 1\\cdot {v}_{0}^{2} \\\\ 12 & =\\frac{49}{2}-\\frac{1}{2}{v}_{0}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}{v}_{0}^{2} & =\\frac{25}{2} \\\\ {v}_{0}^{2} & =25 \\\\ {v}_{0} & =\\pm 5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can disregard the negative solution since the velocity and acceleration of the particle are positive in the regions under consideration. \n\nTherefore, the velocity of the particle at $x=0$ is $5\\,m/s.$\n\nFinally, the particle's change in velocity from $x=0$ to $x=2$ is\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}-{v}_{0}=7-5=2\\,m/s.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389463 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "226918d0-485c-57fb-b37f-551c971f4927" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389465} {:answer/id #uuid "49371fc0-7c0a-5176-b84d-8fdac3f9bd9f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{6}\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389466} {:answer/id #uuid "4e01aaf7-2644-59d0-beea-cbe8e4f810f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{21}{8}\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389467} {:answer/id #uuid "6ec4407e-4e50-5120-b041-d1dbb0df353d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{15}{4}\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389468} {:answer/id #uuid "13bff32a-258c-58a1-a056-0f3af9f9b3f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389469}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4e01aaf7-2644-59d0-beea-cbe8e4f810f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{21}{8}\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389467} :answer-field/id #uuid "5b2247e4-31f8-5907-8437-cf47cd59d1d4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389464}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "f633b7bf-41b1-55e5-94fa-734d0d539fd4" :question/math-academy-id "q-163711" :question/problem "A particle $P$ of mass $3\\,kg$ moves along the $x$-axis from rest at the origin with an acceleration of magnitude $\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2}\\,{m/s}^{2},$ where the acceleration acts in the direction of increasing $x,$ and $t\\ge 0$ is the time in seconds. Calculate the change in kinetic energy of the particle between the times $t=1\\,s$ and $t=2\\,s.$" :question/worked-solution "The change in kinetic energy of a particle with mass $m,$ initial velocity ${v}_{0},$ and final velocity ${v}_{1}$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔ\\,\\text{KE}=\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{1}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{0}^{2}.\n$$\n\nWhen the mass and velocity are measured in kilograms and meters per second, respectively, the particle's kinetic energy is measured in Joules ($J$).\n\nFirst, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ v(t) & =\\int \\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ v(t) & =\\frac{1}{6}{t}^{3}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C,$ we use the fact that $v(0)=0.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n0=\\frac{1}{6}({0}^{3})+C\\,⟹\\,C=0.\n$$\n\nHence, the velocity of the particle at time $t$ is $v(t)=\\frac{1}{6}{t}^{3}.$ In particular, its inital velocity at time $t=1\\,s$ and final velocity at time $t=2\\,s$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{0} & =v(1)=\\frac{1}{6}({1}^{3})=\\frac{1}{6}\\,m/s, \\\\ {v}_{1} & =v(2)=\\frac{1}{6}({2}^{3})=\\frac{4}{3}\\,m/s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the change in kinetic energy of the particle between these times is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ\\,\\text{KE} & =\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{1}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 3\\cdot {\\left(\\frac{4}{3}\\right)}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 3\\cdot {\\left(\\frac{1}{6}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{3}-\\frac{1}{24} \\\\  & =\\frac{21}{8}\\,J.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389470 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "90ca48dc-9ebd-5ddb-b0fd-4e9e4bf3e523" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389472} {:answer/id #uuid "bb17431a-f15d-57b4-8155-d193fd52d02a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389473} {:answer/id #uuid "1341ffcc-a761-51c7-8882-f9f94717f134" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389474} {:answer/id #uuid "760e497b-11bf-5399-b595-62bbffc2cba4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "36\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389475} {:answer/id #uuid "4f138711-dd5f-59c8-bf2b-72bb46435286" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "27\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389476}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4f138711-dd5f-59c8-bf2b-72bb46435286" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "27\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389476} :answer-field/id #uuid "5f0ec5fc-0f7c-5acb-8a13-4ea7ffbac1a6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389471}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "8c495a30-23c5-530b-9d91-e188ad9ee72e" :question/math-academy-id "q-163714" :question/problem "A particle $P$ of mass $\\frac{8}{5}\\,kg$ moves along the $x$-axis from rest at the origin with an acceleration of magnitude $3t\\,{m/s}^{2},$ where the acceleration acts in the direction of increasing $x,$ and $t\\ge 0$ is the time in seconds. Calculate the change in kinetic energy of the particle between the times $t=1\\,s$ and $t=2\\,s.$" :question/worked-solution "The change in kinetic energy of a particle with mass $m,$ initial velocity ${v}_{0},$ and final velocity ${v}_{1}$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔ\\,\\text{KE}=\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{1}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{0}^{2}.\n$$\n\nWhen the mass and velocity are measured in kilograms and meters per second, respectively, the particle's kinetic energy is measured in Joules ($J$).\n\nFirst, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ v(t) & =\\int 3t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ v(t) & =\\frac{3}{2}{t}^{2}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C,$ we use the fact that $v(0)=0.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n0=\\frac{3}{2}({0}^{2})+C\\,⟹\\,C=0.\n$$\n\nHence, the velocity of the particle at time $t$ is $v(t)=\\frac{3}{2}{t}^{2}.$ In particular, its inital velocity at time $t=1\\,s$ and final velocity at time $t=2\\,s$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{0} & =v(1)=\\frac{3}{2}({1}^{2})=\\frac{3}{2}\\,m/s, \\\\ {v}_{1} & =v(2)=\\frac{3}{2}({2}^{2})=6\\,m/s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the change in kinetic energy of the particle between these times is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ\\,\\text{KE} & =\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{1}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{8}{5}\\cdot {6}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{8}{5}\\cdot {\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{144}{5}-\\frac{9}{5} \\\\  & =27\\,J.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416257 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2a35d382-4f29-517d-8811-38dd718f3949" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416152} {:answer/id #uuid "6a609468-13c4-5167-adfa-d9641c742919" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{17}{4}\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416174} {:answer/id #uuid "7fb1a335-16df-592b-9aa2-30cdc25d7546" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{4}\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416185} {:answer/id #uuid "9c755c70-2778-53b1-aa2d-fd52ee0d754b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{2}\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416197} {:answer/id #uuid "d8495461-fbf1-5db5-90d6-ecc9dfa46e3c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416225}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6a609468-13c4-5167-adfa-d9641c742919" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{17}{4}\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416174} :answer-field/id #uuid "ecf8557a-f127-5151-95f4-647459864ef1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416254}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "0be1c169-3c4d-5e81-bcd5-f050f3ef61f9" :question/math-academy-id "q-163687" :question/problem "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis under the action of a force of magnitude $(8-{x}^{3})\\,\\text{N}$ acting in the direction of increasing $x.$ Given that $x$ is measured in meters, calculate the work done by the force in moving the particle from $x=1$ to $x=2.$" :question/worked-solution "The work done by a force $F=F(x)$ in moving a particle from $x={x}_{1}$ to $x={x}_{2}$ is given by\n\n$$\nW={\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}F(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWhen the force and distance are measured in Newtons and meters, respectively, the work done by the force is measured in Joules ($J$).\n\nSubstituting the given values into our integral, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}(8-{x}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[8x-\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{4}]}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =8(2)-\\frac{1}{4}({2}^{4})-(8(1)-\\frac{1}{4}({1}^{4})) \\\\  & =16-4-8+\\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{17}{4}\\,J.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416258 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4ec6c244-5362-5ac9-9cb2-e52010f2e97e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "32\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416164} {:answer/id #uuid "562735b2-f2b9-5808-9e01-b4da0083cca1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "36\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416168} {:answer/id #uuid "6a368a44-5bac-522d-9eaa-44cf19404a5e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416173} {:answer/id #uuid "c924bbae-ceed-5733-99b9-207c3ea0be7d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416218} {:answer/id #uuid "e23afd90-794c-56ba-9d78-eb6940eb6376" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "27\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416228}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e23afd90-794c-56ba-9d78-eb6940eb6376" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "27\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416228} :answer-field/id #uuid "d5e2fc07-70cb-5248-ae29-9ab2d781882c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416251}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "115c519a-042d-5002-84f6-473dd06e56ff" :question/math-academy-id "q-163709" :question/problem "A particle $P$ of mass $\\frac{3}{2}\\,kg$ moves along the $x$-axis from rest at the origin with an acceleration of magnitude $(1+2t)\\,{m/s}^{2},$ where the acceleration acts in the direction of increasing $x,$ and $t\\ge 0$ is the time in seconds. Calculate the particle's kinetic energy at time $t=2\\,s.$" :question/worked-solution "The kinetic energy of a particle with mass $m$ and velocity $v$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{KE}=\\frac{1}{2}m{v}^{2}.\n$$\n\nWhen the mass and velocity are measured in kilograms and meters per second, respectively, the particle's kinetic energy is measured in Joules ($J$).\n\nFirst, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ v(t) & =\\int (1+2t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ v(t) & =t+{t}^{2}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C,$ we use the fact that $v(0)=0.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n0=0+{0}^{2}+C\\,⟹\\,C=0.\n$$\n\nHence, the velocity of the particle at time $t$ is $v(t)=t+{t}^{2}.$ In particular, its velocity at time $t=2\\,s$ is\n\n$$\nv=v(2)=2+{2}^{2}=6\\,m/s.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the kinetic energy of the particle at $t=2$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{KE} & =\\frac{1}{2}m{v}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}\\cdot {6}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}\\cdot 36 \\\\  & =27\\,J.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416259 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0fd6aeb4-c566-5a88-9e32-388d6c8dfea3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{ln}^{2}⁡(2)\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416145} {:answer/id #uuid "6ace19a1-23ee-5f18-97e5-036b1b6c5ceb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}{ln}^{2}⁡(2)\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416175} {:answer/id #uuid "87c5b30a-40f0-5260-b4b3-8c31092ef6f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{ln}^{2}⁡(2)\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416188} {:answer/id #uuid "a71705a5-1eed-57a7-9c98-5053c71a7ae2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}{ln}^{2}⁡(2)\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416202} {:answer/id #uuid "bca90054-3073-5e42-9916-d9655036d407" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6{ln}^{2}⁡(2)\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416212}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bca90054-3073-5e42-9916-d9655036d407" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6{ln}^{2}⁡(2)\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416212} :answer-field/id #uuid "d1a23531-d7b7-5e92-b4ab-f45652f32a5c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416250}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "2dec6491-4f2e-5985-9f0d-5f140c0cfaf3" :question/math-academy-id "q-163713" :question/problem "A particle $P$ of mass $4\\,kg$ moves along the $x$-axis from rest at the origin with an acceleration of magnitude $\\frac{1}{t+1}\\,{m/s}^{2},$ where the acceleration acts in the direction of increasing $x,$ and $t\\ge 0$ is the time in seconds. Calculate the change in kinetic energy of the particle between the times $t=1\\,s$ and $t=3\\,s.$" :question/worked-solution "The change in kinetic energy of a particle with mass $m,$ initial velocity ${v}_{0},$ and final velocity ${v}_{1}$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔ\\,\\text{KE}=\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{1}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{0}^{2}.\n$$\n\nWhen the mass and velocity are measured in kilograms and meters per second, respectively, the particle's kinetic energy is measured in Joules ($J$).\n\nFirst, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ v(t) & =\\int \\frac{1}{t+1}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ v(t) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|t+1|+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C,$ we use the fact that $v(0)=0.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n0=\\operatorname{ln}⁡|0+1|+C\\,⟹\\,C=0.\n$$\n\nHence, the velocity of the particle at time $t$ is $v(t)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡|t+1|.$ In particular, its inital velocity at time $t=1\\,s$ and final velocity at time $t=3\\,s$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{0} & =v(1)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡|1+1|=\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\,m/s, \\\\ {v}_{1} & =v(3)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡|3+1|=2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\,m/s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the change in kinetic energy of the particle between these times is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ\\,\\text{KE} & =\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{1}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 4\\cdot {(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 4\\cdot {(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)}^{2} \\\\  & =8{ln}^{2}⁡2-2{ln}^{2}⁡2 \\\\  & =6{ln}^{2}⁡(2)\\,J.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416260 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "245f5f16-c1db-52b8-93a7-c27b81880f3f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "625\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416150} {:answer/id #uuid "357620ce-cca0-59e5-a668-2ee27ee5034a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "735\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416154} {:answer/id #uuid "3f12cf32-2fc6-5ed9-9aec-bed26735d3c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "660\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416156} {:answer/id #uuid "8cb1f837-d5d5-5a17-b24a-c680b3267dfe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "585\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416190} {:answer/id #uuid "cd33b190-ed48-5fec-9293-b65a62708603" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "530\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416220}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8cb1f837-d5d5-5a17-b24a-c680b3267dfe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "585\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416190} :answer-field/id #uuid "9bd8f27e-7cc0-5e2f-951e-b5461e6ef25c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416247}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "34ad571d-a040-5724-a4dd-5d1ff6b4a0c6" :question/math-academy-id "q-163674" :question/problem "A particle $P$ of mass $2\\,kg$ moves along the $x$-axis under the action of a resultant force. The displacement of the particle is given by $({t}^{3}+2)\\,m,$ where $t\\ge 0$ is the time in seconds. Calculate the work done by the force between the times $t=2\\,s$ and $t=3\\,s.$" :question/worked-solution "The work-energy principle states that the work done by a resultant force on a particle equals the change in the particle's kinetic energy. That is,\n\n$$\nW=Δ\\,\\text{KE}\n$$\n\nwhere the change in kinetic energy of a particle is given by\n\n$$\nΔ\\,\\text{KE}=\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{1}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{0}^{2}\n$$\n\nand $m$ is the particle's mass, ${v}_{0}$ is its initial velocity, and ${v}_{1}$ is its final velocity.\n\nThe displacement of the particle is given by $x(t)={t}^{3}+2,$ where $t$ is time in seconds.\nWe find the velocity $v(t)$ of the particle by differentiating the displacement with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3}+2) \\\\  & =3{t}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the particle's initial velocity, at $t=2$, is\n\n$$\n{v}_{0}=v(2)=3(2)^{2}=12\\,m/s,\n$$\n\nand final velocity, at $t=3$, is\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=v(3)=3(3)^{2}=27\\,m/s.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the work-energy principle, the work done by the force between these times is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W & =Δ\\,\\text{KE} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2\\cdot (27)^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2\\cdot (12)^{2} \\\\  & =729-144 \\\\  & =585\\,J.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416261 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6e01e9e2-099f-5829-bb90-988331fa2c4e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416178} {:answer/id #uuid "944d02a9-bd37-50e5-8f3a-13e10492e0a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416195} {:answer/id #uuid "afa584ba-d9c9-5af5-875d-b8761e92e385" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "22\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416203} {:answer/id #uuid "bf2651a2-6940-5df5-b549-821bd938e2ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416213} {:answer/id #uuid "d88f3206-b7d8-5454-a54b-c8dbf5728dd6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416226}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bf2651a2-6940-5df5-b549-821bd938e2ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416213} :answer-field/id #uuid "e533a5eb-946c-53fd-b9db-7dca4ec6140a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416253}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "49e23a3c-f5ee-5f32-8207-650dc0b4ca07" :question/math-academy-id "q-163686" :question/problem "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis under the action of a force of magnitude $(3\\sqrt{x}+2)\\,\\text{N}$ acting in the direction of increasing $x.$ Given that $x$ is measured in meters, calculate the work done by the force in moving the particle from $x=1$ to $x=4.$" :question/worked-solution "The work done by a force $F=F(x)$ in moving a particle from $x={x}_{1}$ to $x={x}_{2}$ is given by\n\n$$\nW={\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}F(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWhen the force and distance are measured in Newtons and meters, respectively, the work done by the force is measured in Joules ($J$).\n\nSubstituting the given values into our integral, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}(3\\sqrt{x}+2)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}(3{x}^{1/2}+2)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[2{x}^{3/2}+2x]}_{1}^{4} \\\\  & =2({4}^{3/2})+2(4)-(2({1}^{3/2})+2(1)) \\\\  & =16+8-2-2 \\\\  & =20\\,J.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416262 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1ed38dcf-d565-5c8f-a354-7f15129024b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416148} {:answer/id #uuid "4906765c-cf5e-56de-b7cc-bbfeff0afa63" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416162} {:answer/id #uuid "78e2fabc-a95e-59e5-9e2c-304ec9d164ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416182} {:answer/id #uuid "7e2f1ede-2cc2-54bb-8896-41a7f39dd5ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{4}\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416184} {:answer/id #uuid "fc464ecd-6249-5343-9b2d-d1ebf3f3d163" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{4}\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416237}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7e2f1ede-2cc2-54bb-8896-41a7f39dd5ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{4}\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416184} :answer-field/id #uuid "6d665a14-f9c6-5b0e-9f42-6dea1fa5cd93" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416245}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "539e0059-24fe-5c3a-b9b6-abd2934de330" :question/math-academy-id "q-163688" :question/problem "A particle $P$ of mass $0.5\\,kg$ moves along the $x$-axis with an acceleration of magnitude $(5-x)\\,{m/s}^{2}$ acting in the direction $OP.$ Given that $x$ is measured in meters, calculate the work done by the force in moving the particle from $x=1$ to $x=2.$" :question/worked-solution "The work done by a force $F=F(x)$ in moving a particle from $x={x}_{1}$ to $x={x}_{2}$ is given by\n\n$$\nW={\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}F(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWhen the force and distance are measured in Newtons and meters, respectively, the work done by the force is measured in Joules ($J$).\n\nUsing Newton's second law of motion, the force required to accelerate a particle of mass $0.5\\,kg$ at a rate of $(5-x)\\,{m/s}^{2}$ is\n\n$$\nF=ma=0.5\\cdot (5-x)=(2.5-0.5x)\\,N.\n$$\n\nNow, substituting the known values into our integral, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}(2.5-0.5x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{5}{2}x-\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{2}]}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}(2)-\\frac{1}{4}({2}^{2})-(\\frac{5}{2}(1)-\\frac{1}{4}({1}^{2})) \\\\  & =5-1-\\frac{5}{2}+\\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{4}\\,J.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416263 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "583dd30f-780b-569e-92c3-cc4ee471b53b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "96\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416169} {:answer/id #uuid "9ec9126b-bae7-5d30-87eb-6515e3d60873" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "118\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416198} {:answer/id #uuid "b711d539-6b80-51e9-bad0-dc087d68179d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "103\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416209} {:answer/id #uuid "cd3cb8b5-896e-5b5e-a89a-10510757b28c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "85\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416221} {:answer/id #uuid "d4a04a41-69d2-572c-85f9-3f8ecf5abeb1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "90\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416224}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d4a04a41-69d2-572c-85f9-3f8ecf5abeb1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "90\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416224} :answer-field/id #uuid "440ab6c6-89d8-5d73-9c6d-94b052c4135f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416243}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "6813bd80-c67c-56a5-8d0d-2e4e5ee1b618" :question/math-academy-id "q-163672" :question/problem "A particle $P$ of mass $3\\,kg$ moves along the $x$-axis under the action of a resultant force. The particle's velocity is $(3t-1)\\,m/s,$ where $t\\ge 0$ is the time in seconds. Calculate the work done by the force between the times $t=1\\,s$ and $t=3\\,s.$" :question/worked-solution "The work-energy principle states that the work done by a resultant force on a particle equals the change in the particle's kinetic energy. That is,\n\n$$\nW=Δ\\,\\text{KE}\n$$\n\nwhere the change in kinetic energy of a particle is given by\n\n$$\nΔ\\,\\text{KE}=\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{1}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{0}^{2}\n$$\n\nand $m$ is the particle's mass, ${v}_{0}$ is its initial velocity, and ${v}_{1}$ is its final velocity.\n\nThe particle's velocity is given by $v(t)=3t-1,$ where $t$ is time in seconds. So, the particle's initial velocity, at $t=1$, is\n\n$$\n{v}_{0}=v(1)=3(1)-1=2\\,m/s,\n$$\n\nand final velocity, at $t=3$, is\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=v(3)=3(3)-1=8\\,m/s.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the work-energy principle, the work done by the force between these times is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W & =Δ\\,\\text{KE} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 3\\cdot (8)^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 3\\cdot (2)^{2} \\\\  & =96-6 \\\\  & =90\\,J.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416264 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "016d8147-2b48-53a6-82af-fafe816ab49a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416143} {:answer/id #uuid "4e46b4a9-a4e3-5f5f-80ed-268038e11e48" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416163} {:answer/id #uuid "8f693876-d062-56a4-8e01-a7786aea86f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416191} {:answer/id #uuid "9eea50ba-01cb-55aa-9a12-e59c66a5bac2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416199} {:answer/id #uuid "e9d6d1ac-3382-5896-9f56-457c7886e3bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416230}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "016d8147-2b48-53a6-82af-fafe816ab49a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416143} :answer-field/id #uuid "33c85393-49c2-59b3-86c8-fb54c6c913c0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416242}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "7a6ac8ce-563c-538e-a4b6-829308c43be7" :question/math-academy-id "q-163706" :question/problem "A particle $P$ of mass $3\\,kg$ moves along the $x$-axis from rest at the origin with an acceleration of magnitude $3\\sqrt{t}\\,{m/s}^{2},$ where the acceleration acts in the direction of increasing $x,$ and $t\\ge 0$ is the time in seconds. Calculate the particle's kinetic energy at time $t=1\\,s.$" :question/worked-solution "The kinetic energy of a particle with mass $m$ and velocity $v$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{KE}=\\frac{1}{2}m{v}^{2}.\n$$\n\nWhen the mass and velocity are measured in kilograms and meters per second, respectively, the particle's kinetic energy is measured in Joules ($J$).\n\nFirst, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ v(t) & =\\int 3\\sqrt{t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ v(t) & =2{t}^{3/2}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C,$ we use the fact that $v(0)=0.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n0=2({0}^{3/2})+C\\,⟹\\,C=0.\n$$\n\nHence, the velocity of the particle at time $t$ is $v(t)=2{t}^{3/2}.$ In particular, its velocity at time $t=1\\,s$ is\n\n$$\nv=v(1)=2({1}^{3/2})=2\\,m/s.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the kinetic energy of the particle at $t=1$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{KE} & =\\frac{1}{2}m{v}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 3\\cdot {2}^{2} \\\\  & =6\\,J.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416265 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "31c287bc-7b24-588a-a540-58a1e566a1b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1\\,m/s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416153} {:answer/id #uuid "6f79dc59-3bf2-54c9-9f6d-145c24052071" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\,m/s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416179} {:answer/id #uuid "76dbeeb4-610b-5130-aaee-b66603d18018" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\,m/s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416181} {:answer/id #uuid "98f49da7-f783-5dd3-b704-89fad4575d51" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\,m/s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416196} {:answer/id #uuid "cc674083-0796-5e90-b9c6-07a3221072a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\,m/s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416219}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6f79dc59-3bf2-54c9-9f6d-145c24052071" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\,m/s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416179} :answer-field/id #uuid "29c62bd1-d614-5073-873e-5b53441e5434" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416241}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "841964b0-d48f-583c-9b1a-d5d38816deaf" :question/math-academy-id "q-163666" :question/problem "A particle $P$ of mass $4\\,kg$ moves along the $x$-axis under the action of the force of magnitude $(4{x}^{3}+6{x}^{2})\\,N$ acting in the direction of increasing $x.$ Given that distances along the $x$-axis are measured in meters, the particle is initially at the origin, and the particle has velocity $v=5\\,m/s$ at $x=2,$ find the particle's change in velocity in moving from $x=0$ to $x=2.$" :question/worked-solution "The work-energy principle states that the work done by a resultant force on a particle equals the change in the particle's kinetic energy. That is,\n\n$$\nW=Δ\\,\\text{KE}\n$$\n\nwhere the change in kinetic energy of a particle is given by\n\n$$\nΔ\\,\\text{KE}=\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{1}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{0}^{2}\n$$\n\nand $m$ is the particle's mass, ${v}_{0}$ is its initial velocity, and ${v}_{1}$ is its final velocity.\n\nFirst, we calculate the work done by the force in moving the particle from $x=0$ to $x=2:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W & ={\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}F(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(4{x}^{3}+6{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[{x}^{4}+2{x}^{3}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =16+16-0 \\\\  & =32\\,J\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe particle has velocity $v=5\\,m/s$ at $x=2.$ So, we can find the velocity ${v}_{0}$ at $x=0$ using the work-energy principle:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W & =\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{1}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{0}^{2} \\\\ 32 & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 4\\cdot {5}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 4\\cdot {v}_{0}^{2} \\\\ 32 & =50-2{v}_{0}^{2} \\\\ 2{v}_{0}^{2} & =18 \\\\ {v}_{0}^{2} & =9 \\\\ {v}_{0} & =\\pm 3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can disregard the negative solution since the velocity and acceleration of the particle are positive in the regions under consideration. \n\nTherefore, the velocity of the particle at $x=0$ is $3\\,m/s.$\n\nFinally, the particle's change in velocity from $x=0$ to $x=2$ is\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}-{v}_{0}=5-3=2\\,m/s.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416266 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4688ab13-3768-5564-a3fb-bdcb6748fe3d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{2}\\,m/s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416159} {:answer/id #uuid "542437df-028e-59d6-ae86-f7227217dfb1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\,m/s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416167} {:answer/id #uuid "673d5641-d0ad-5ff6-a052-0f9b30a96ae7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{4}\\,m/s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416172} {:answer/id #uuid "70095663-b0c7-5784-aa18-5da9b912ed20" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1\\,m/s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416180} {:answer/id #uuid "a42ed274-a649-5401-8272-60c7780ed481" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\,m/s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416201}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a42ed274-a649-5401-8272-60c7780ed481" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\,m/s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416201} :answer-field/id #uuid "a9005e61-607d-5045-aa66-20814a697f93" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416248}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "a80bea3f-8cb9-59ff-8c63-3325a8f0a338" :question/math-academy-id "q-163703" :question/problem "A particle $P$ of mass $\\frac{1}{2}\\,kg$ moves along the $x$-axis under the action of the force of magnitude $(3-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})\\,N$ acting in the direction of increasing $x.$ Given that distances along the $x$-axis are measured in meters, the particle is initially at $x=1,$ and the particle has velocity $v=7\\,m/s$ at $x=4,$ find the particle's change in velocity in moving from $x=1$ to $x=4.$" :question/worked-solution "The work-energy principle states that the work done by a resultant force on a particle equals the change in the particle's kinetic energy. That is,\n\n$$\nW=Δ\\,\\text{KE}\n$$\n\nwhere the change in kinetic energy of a particle is given by\n\n$$\nΔ\\,\\text{KE}=\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{1}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{0}^{2}\n$$\n\nand $m$ is the particle's mass, ${v}_{0}$ is its initial velocity, and ${v}_{1}$ is its final velocity.\n\nFirst, we calculate the work done by the force in moving the particle from $x=1$ to $x=4:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W & ={\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}F(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}(3-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[3x+\\frac{1}{x}]}_{1}^{4} \\\\  & =(12+\\frac{1}{4})-(3+1) \\\\  & =\\frac{33}{4}\\,J\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe particle has velocity $v=7\\,m/s$ at $x=4.$ So, we can find the velocity ${v}_{0}$ at $x=1$ using the work-energy principle:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W & =\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{1}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{0}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{33}{4} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {7}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {v}_{0}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{33}{4} & =\\frac{49}{4}-\\frac{1}{4}{v}_{0}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{4}{v}_{0}^{2} & =4 \\\\ {v}_{0}^{2} & =16 \\\\ {v}_{0} & =\\pm 4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can disregard the negative solution since the velocity and acceleration of the particle are positive in the regions under consideration. \n\nTherefore, the velocity of the particle at $x=1$ is $4\\,m/s.$\n\nFinally, the particle's change in velocity from $x=1$ to $x=4$ is\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}-{v}_{0}=7-4=3\\,m/s.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416267 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4f15786c-7b03-54e6-8703-5ed8327eeb6f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416165} {:answer/id #uuid "917167c2-f28e-5235-8996-88a317199397" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416193} {:answer/id #uuid "bb2d71f8-f520-5950-a0fc-eaf5e6853af6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416211} {:answer/id #uuid "e9148945-fdec-553f-9907-9d2484cbc0ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416229} {:answer/id #uuid "f75d04ef-9f29-5e3a-9b0c-022da6954dc5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416235}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "917167c2-f28e-5235-8996-88a317199397" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416193} :answer-field/id #uuid "f0260c4e-12d5-5629-b2d4-7c1539134366" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416256}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "cc2329ee-2286-5cc1-b9c4-aaae4fcfd160" :question/math-academy-id "q-163682" :question/problem "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis under the action of a force of magnitude $(2-x)\\,\\text{N}$ acting in the direction of increasing $x.$ Given that $x$ is measured in meters, calculate the work done by the force in moving the particle from $x=1$ to $x=2.$" :question/worked-solution "The work done by a force $F=F(x)$ in moving a particle from $x={x}_{1}$ to $x={x}_{2}$ is given by\n\n$$\nW={\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}F(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWhen the force and distance are measured in Newtons and meters, respectively, the work done by the force is measured in Joules ($J$).\n\nSubstituting the given values into our integral, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}(2-x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[2x-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}]}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =2(2)-\\frac{1}{2}({2}^{2})-(2(1)-\\frac{1}{2}({1}^{2})) \\\\  & =4-2-2+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\,J.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416268 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6d7dfd5d-6a26-57a3-9520-0e5f3f2556d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "95\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416177} {:answer/id #uuid "9f10d917-ef03-5eb9-9451-45b13e37761c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "120\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416200} {:answer/id #uuid "d47e9cdf-700c-5733-a11d-78b0ff44a038" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "72\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416223} {:answer/id #uuid "dda5a39a-b3d7-53c3-a43b-5f036f3bb6db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "64\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416227} {:answer/id #uuid "efd1ddb6-ca2b-5d57-8cbb-36a4723bcb03" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "108\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416233}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d47e9cdf-700c-5733-a11d-78b0ff44a038" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "72\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416223} :answer-field/id #uuid "e2a61099-7c6c-5889-a8f0-36c27d613946" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416252}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "d148a18c-482f-58b6-ae26-c16e793a32c1" :question/math-academy-id "q-163676" :question/problem "A particle $P$ of mass $0.25\\,kg$ moves along the $x$-axis under the action of a resultant force. The displacement of the particle is given by $({t}^{4}-2{t}^{2}+2)\\,m,$ where $t\\ge 0$ is the time in seconds. Calculate the work done by the force between the times $t=1\\,s$ and $t=2\\,s.$" :question/worked-solution "The work-energy principle states that the work done by a resultant force on a particle equals the change in the particle's kinetic energy. That is,\n\n$$\nW=Δ\\,\\text{KE}\n$$\n\nwhere the change in kinetic energy of a particle is given by\n\n$$\nΔ\\,\\text{KE}=\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{1}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{0}^{2}\n$$\n\nand $m$ is the particle's mass, ${v}_{0}$ is its initial velocity, and ${v}_{1}$ is its final velocity.\n\nThe displacement of the particle is given by $x(t)={t}^{4}-2{t}^{2}+2,$ where $t$ is time in seconds.\nWe find the velocity $v(t)$ of the particle by differentiating the displacement with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{4}-2{t}^{2}+2) \\\\  & =4{t}^{3}-4t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the particle's initial velocity, at $t=1$, is\n\n$$\n{v}_{0}=v(1)=4(1)^{3}-4(1)=0\\,m/s,\n$$\n\nand final velocity, at $t=2$, is\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=v(2)=4(2)^{3}-4(2)=24\\,m/s.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the work-energy principle, the work done by the force between these times is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W & =Δ\\,\\text{KE} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 0.25\\cdot (24)^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 0.25\\cdot {0}^{2} \\\\  & =72-0 \\\\  & =72\\,J.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416269 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "23a970a6-fde1-5b7d-91a5-9f491fb2d37a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\,m/s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416149} {:answer/id #uuid "4862b45c-aff1-5dd0-bdea-a37244d8264e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8\\,m/s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416161} {:answer/id #uuid "4f969a37-7f40-5d87-a34d-979e387d5920" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}\\,m/s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416166} {:answer/id #uuid "b4ddfe8f-5177-5ba3-bcf9-6389f879b188" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{2}\\,m/s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416206} {:answer/id #uuid "b63eb1f1-da1c-50d8-9fa0-a62c1a301b08" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\,m/s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416208}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b4ddfe8f-5177-5ba3-bcf9-6389f879b188" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{2}\\,m/s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416206} :answer-field/id #uuid "03c056e2-1426-5bcb-a501-ebc6f3a55d75" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416238}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "e08604c1-4714-50ad-b1df-f5621c3586f5" :question/math-academy-id "q-163691" :question/problem "A particle $P$ of mass $4\\,kg$ moves along the $x$-axis under the action of a force of magnitude $3{x}^{2}\\,N$ acting in the direction of increasing $x.$ Given that $x$ is measured in meters, and the particle has velocity $v=2\\,m/s$ at $x=0,$ calculate the velocity of the particle at $x=2.$" :question/worked-solution "The work-energy principle states that the work done by a resultant force on a particle equals the change in the particle's kinetic energy. That is,\n\n$$\nW=Δ\\,\\text{KE}\n$$\n\nwhere the change in kinetic energy of a particle is given by\n\n$$\nΔ\\,\\text{KE}=\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{1}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{0}^{2}\n$$\n\nand $m$ is the particle's mass, ${v}_{0}$ is its initial velocity, and ${v}_{1}$ is its final velocity.\n\nFirst, we calculate the work done by the force in moving the particle from $x=0$ to $x=2:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W & ={\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}F(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}3{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={x}^{3}{|}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & ={2}^{3}-0 \\\\  & =8\\,J\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe particle has velocity $v=2\\,m/s$ at $x=0.$ So, we can find the velocity ${v}_{1}$ at $x=2$ using the work-energy principle:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W & =\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{1}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{0}^{2} \\\\ 8 & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 4\\cdot {v}_{1}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 4\\cdot {2}^{2} \\\\ 8 & =2{v}_{1}^{2}-8 \\\\ 2{v}_{1}^{2} & =16 \\\\ {v}_{1}^{2} & =8 \\\\ {v}_{1} & =\\pm 2\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can disregard the negative solution since the velocity and acceleration of the particle are positive in the regions under consideration. \n\nTherefore, the velocity of the particle at $x=2$ is $2\\sqrt{2}\\,m/s.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416270 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "44790b19-9ced-5d46-bcb9-825594809176" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416157} {:answer/id #uuid "6215fee1-a677-5186-a3d4-62cb7223b14c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416171} {:answer/id #uuid "b234e6b8-43be-5423-be11-ece81f246d15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{4}\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416204} {:answer/id #uuid "b60dbd39-c84e-5d27-aee9-c2f06ea806f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416207} {:answer/id #uuid "d1b9a351-f8a5-58dc-901e-b298299b5029" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416222}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b234e6b8-43be-5423-be11-ece81f246d15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{4}\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416204} :answer-field/id #uuid "915e3c86-f8a0-5f78-befd-32698839d7d2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416246}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "fdc532fe-5376-523b-b4c1-4852cb3ef3ca" :question/math-academy-id "q-163704" :question/problem "A particle $P$ of mass $8\\,kg$ moves along the $x$-axis from rest at the origin with an acceleration of magnitude $\\frac{1}{(t+1)^{2}}\\,{m/s}^{2},$ where the acceleration acts in the direction of increasing $x,$ and $t\\ge 0$ is the time in seconds. Calculate the particle's kinetic energy at time $t=3\\,s.$" :question/worked-solution "The kinetic energy of a particle with mass $m$ and velocity $v$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{KE}=\\frac{1}{2}m{v}^{2}.\n$$\n\nWhen the mass and velocity are measured in kilograms and meters per second, respectively, the particle's kinetic energy is measured in Joules ($J$).\n\nFirst, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ v(t) & =\\int \\frac{1}{(t+1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ v(t) & =-\\frac{1}{t+1}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C,$ we use the fact that $v(0)=0.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n0=-\\frac{1}{0+1}+C\\,⟹\\,C=1.\n$$\n\nHence, the velocity of the particle at time $t$ is $v(t)=1-\\frac{1}{t+1}.$ In particular, its velocity at time $t=3\\,s$ is\n\n$$\nv=v(3)=1-\\frac{1}{3+1}=\\frac{3}{4}\\,m/s.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the kinetic energy of the particle at $t=3$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{KE} & =\\frac{1}{2}m{v}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 8\\cdot {\\left(\\frac{3}{4}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 8\\cdot \\frac{9}{16} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{4}\\,J.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427393 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "005f40cc-1636-56ab-b246-5737a11e79cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427387} {:answer/id #uuid "49730251-5fc6-518d-bdf0-26723df97b08" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{4}\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427388} {:answer/id #uuid "5e6cfef4-3fa0-536d-accc-16bd9fddb64c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427389} {:answer/id #uuid "a1bab12d-028f-5c7b-8260-e0dd0f56b76b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{15}{2}\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427390} {:answer/id #uuid "ce34ff78-907d-5c9a-b016-26b849102736" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{20}{3}\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427391}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ce34ff78-907d-5c9a-b016-26b849102736" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{20}{3}\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427391} :answer-field/id #uuid "7df70823-9962-541f-9d3b-9f6bc58730dc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427392}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "720a885d-9cf4-5861-8b99-013e8190ec62" :question/math-academy-id "q-163683" :question/problem "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis under the action of a force of magnitude $(2x+{x}^{2})\\,\\text{N}$ acting in the direction of increasing $x.$ Given that $x$ is measured in meters, calculate the work done by the force in moving the particle from $x=0$ to $x=2.$" :question/worked-solution "The work done by a force $F=F(x)$ in moving a particle from $x={x}_{1}$ to $x={x}_{2}$ is given by\n\n$$\nW={\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}F(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWhen the force and distance are measured in Newtons and meters, respectively, the work done by the force is measured in Joules ($J$).\n\nSubstituting the given values into our integral, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(2x+{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[{x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & ={2}^{2}+\\frac{1}{3}({2}^{3})-({0}^{2}+\\frac{1}{3}({0}^{3})) \\\\  & =4+\\frac{8}{3}-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{20}{3}\\,J.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
